window.CET4_BANKS.push({"id": "2021-12-1", "source": "2021年12月真题·第1套", "writing": {"prompt": "Directions: Suppose your school is organizing an orientation program to help the freshmen adapt to the new environment and academic studies. You are now to write a proposal, which may include its aim, duration, participants and activities. You will have 30 minutes to write the proposal. You should write at least 120 words but no more than 180 words.", "sample": "A Proposal for the Freshmen Orientation Program Our school is planning an orientation program for this year's\nfreshmen, and its main arrangements are proposed below. The program aims to help new students adapt to the\nnew environment and academic studies as smoothly and quickly as possible.\nIn terms of duration, the program will last for one week, starting from the day of registration. As for the\nparticipants, it is open to all incoming freshmen, and it will be organized by the Student Union together with\nvolunteer seniors and several advisers from each department.\nA variety of activities will be arranged during the week. First, campus tours and library workshops will help\nnewcomers find their way around. Second, group meetings with academic advisers will introduce course\nselection and effective study methods. Finally, evening social parties will give students a chance to make new\nfriends and relax.\nWe are confident that this program will give every freshman a warm and confident start. Your approval and\nsupport would be highly appreciated. The Student Union"}, "translation": {"prompt": "大运河 (Grand Canal) 是世界上最长的人工河，北起北京，南至杭州。它是中国历史上最宏伟的工程之一。大运河始建于公元前4世纪，公元13世纪末建成。修建之初是为了运输粮食，后来也用于运输其他商品。大运河沿线区域逐渐发展成为中国的工商业中心。长久以来，大运河对中国的经济发展发挥了重要作用，有力地促进了南北地区之间的人员往来和文化交流。", "sample": "The Grand Canal is the longest man-made river in the world, running from Beijing in the north to Hangzhou in the south. It is one of the most magnificent projects in Chinese history. The construction of the Grand Canal was started in the 4th century BC and completed in the late 13th century. It was originally built to transport grain and was later used to transport other goods as well. The areas along the Grand Canal gradually developed into industrial and commercial centers of China. For a long time, the Grand Canal has played an important role in China's economic development, greatly promoting personnel exchanges and cultural communication between the north and the south."}, "listeningUrl": "https://english-exam.lazynote.cn/cet4/paper/2021-12-1/?f=w", "clozePassage": "Many people believe that passion and commitment are the foundations of strong romantic relationships. But a relationship is made of two  __26__  individuals. And the personality traits(特性) these individuals  __27__  or lack can often make a relationship more or less likely to  __28__  . Recent research has found that one trait in particular—humility(谦逊)—is an important indicator of successful relationships.\nHumility can sometimes be  __29__  with a lack of confidence. But researchers have come to realize that being humble generally indicates the  __30__  of deeply admirable personal qualities. Being humble means you have the ability to accurately  __31__  your deficiencies without denying your skills and strengths. For example, you might recognize that you are intelligent, but realize that you are not a  __32__  . Thus, humility leads to an honest view of one's own advantages and shortcomings. Humble people do not ignore, avoid, or try to deny their limits or deficiencies. They can  __33__  mistakes, see value in things that are far from perfect and identify areas for improvement.\nPerhaps it is not  __34__  , then, that humility appears to be a huge asset to relationships. One study found that people tend to rate this quality  __35__  in their spouse. The study also found that someone who is humble is more likely to initiate a romantic relationship, perhaps because they are less likely to see themselves as \"too good\" for someone else. Thus, a humble partner might be your ideal partner.", "matchIntro": "There's a stress gap between men and women", "matchParagraphs": [{"label": "A", "text": "\"I used to work very hard. I love to create things, grow them and solve problems,\" said Meng Li, a successful app developer in San Francisco. \"I didn't really care about my mind and my body until they decided to go on strike.\""}, {"label": "B", "text": "Ms. Li said her stress led to sleeplessness. When she did sleep, she experienced \"problem-solving dreams,\" which left her feeling unrested when she woke up. \"After I became a first-time mother, I quickly realized I was so busy caring for other people and work that I felt like I'd lost myself,\" she said."}, {"label": "C", "text": "It's a common story—one we frequently ridicule and readily dismiss, for example, by claiming that women tend to complain more than men, despite the growing sum of research that underlines the problem. Women are twice as likely to suffer from severe stress and anxiety as men, according to a 2016 study published in The Journal of Brain & Behavior. The American Psychological Association reports a gender gap year after year showing that women consistently report higher stress levels. Clearly, a stress gap exists."}, {"label": "D", "text": "\"The difference is not really news to me, as a clinical psychologist,\" said Erin Joyce, a women and couples therapist in Los Angeles. \"It's been well documented in extensive research over the years that prevalence rates for the majority of the anxiety disorders are higher in women than men.\" Some people may argue that this is merely reported data, and they say many men feel the same pressures as women in terms of fulfilling responsibilities at work and home. In other words, we're all really, really stressed."}, {"label": "E", "text": "\"The difference, however, is in the nature and scope of these responsibilities in the home environment in particular,\" Dr. Joyce said. For example, the United Nations reported that women do nearly three times as much unpaid domestic work as men. The problem is, housework is often overlooked as work, even though it is often as laborious (or in some cases, more so) as any paid job. As the scholar Silvia Federici put it in 1975, the unpaid nature of domestic work reinforces the assumption that \"housework is not work, thus preventing women from struggling against it.\""}, {"label": "F", "text": "It's not just inside the home, though. Research from Nova Southeastern University found that female managers were more likely than male managers to display \"surface acting,\" or forcing emotions that are not wholly felt. \"They expressed optimism, calmness and sympathy even when these were not the emotions that they were actually feeling,\" the study said."}, {"label": "G", "text": "Surface acting is a prime example of \"emotional labor,\" a concept that the writer Jess Zimmerman made familiar in a 2015 essay. The essay sparked a massive thread on the internet community blog MetaFilter. Hundreds of women spoke up about their own experience with emotional labor: the duties that are expected of them, but go unnoticed. These invisible duties become apparent only when you don't do them. Like domestic labor, emotional labor is generally dismissed and not labeled work. But research shows it can be just as exhausting as paid work. Emotional labor can lead to difficulty in sleeping and family conflict. Sure, circumstantial stress, like losing a job, may lead to these same issues. But emotional labor is not circumstantial. It's an enduring responsibility based on the socialized gender role of women."}, {"label": "H", "text": "Like Ms. Li, many women try to manage the added stress to reach what Dr. Joyce said was an unattainable ideal. \"Some professional women aim to do it all. They want to reach the top of the corporate ladder and fly like supermom,\" she said. When women don't reach this ideal, they feel guilty; and even more stressed. After her own struggle with this, Ms. Li took a step back and used her experience to build Sanity & Self, a self-care app and platform for overworked women. \"The realizations I had in that process helped me gain insights and ultimately got me ready to integrate self-care into my daily life,\" she said."}, {"label": "I", "text": "The stress problem extends beyond mental health when you consider the link between stress, anxiety and heart health. Worse, most of what we know about heart disease comes from studies involving men. However, \"there are many reasons to think that it's different in women,\" Harvard Medical School reported. For example, women are more likely to experience disturbed sleep, anxiety and unusual fatigue before a heart attack. Stress is so normalized that it is easy for women to shrug off those symptoms as simply the consequences of stress. Many women also do not experience chest pain before a heart attack the way men do, which leads to fewer women discovering problematic heart issues. Harvard reports that women are \"much more likely than men to die within a year of having a heart attack\" and \"many women say their physicians sometimes don't even recognize the symptoms.\""}, {"label": "J", "text": "The good news is, women are more likely than men to take charge of their stress and manage it, the American Psychological Association reports. The concept of self-care, at its core, is quite simple. \"The basics of adequate sleep, healthy diet and exercise are a good place to start,\" Dr. Joyce said. \"Support from trusted relationships is vital. This includes professional support from various health and wellness providers if stress is becoming increasingly overwhelming.\""}, {"label": "K", "text": "Disconnecting from work and home responsibilities is also obviously important. But it's much easier said than done. It is important to understand what causes your stress in the first place. \"Get really specific with what's stressing you out,\" Ms. Li said. \"We often chalk up our stress to broad experiences like work. But work stress can take many different forms. Is a colleague being disrespectful of your time? Is a boss undermining your day-to-day control over decision making? These are different causes of stress and can benefit from different kinds of self-care.\""}, {"label": "L", "text": "Ideally, your spouse or partner will be supportive, rather than dismissive, of your stress. It is important to talk through these issues before they come to a head. \"Women working outside of the home should make an effort to have a conscious conversation with their partners about more equitable sharing of household and family responsibilities,\" Dr. Joyce said."}], "readingPassages": {"46": "As many office workers adapt to remote work, cities may undergo fundamental change if offices remain under-utilized. Who will benefit if working from home becomes the norm?\nEmployers argue they make considerable savings on real estate when workers shift from office to home work. However, these savings result from passing costs on to workers.\nUnless employees are fully compensated, this could become a variant of parasitic(寄生的) capitalism, whereby corporate profits increasingly rely on extracting value from the public—and now personal—realm, rather than on generating new value.\nThough employers are backed by a chorus of remote work advocates, others note the loneliness, reduced productivity and inefficiencies of extended remote work.\nIf working from home becomes permanent, employees will have to dedicate part of their private space to work. This requires purchasing desks, chairs and office equipment.\nIt also means having private space dedicated to work: the space must be heated, cleaned, maintained and paid for. That depends on many things, but for purposes of illustration, I have run some estimates for Montreal. The exercise is simple but important, since it brings these costs out of the realm of speculation into the realm of meaningful discussion.\nRough calculations show that the savings made by employers when their staff works from home are of similar value to the compensation workers should receive for setting up offices at home.\nWhat does this mean for offices in cities? One of two things may happen: Employers pass these costs onto employees. This would be a form of expropriation(侵占), with employees absorbing production costs that have traditionally been paid by the employer. This represents a considerable transfer of value from employees to employers.\nWhen employees are properly compensated, employers' real estate savings will be modest. If savings are modest, then the many advantages of working in offices—such as lively atmosphere, rapidity of communication, team-building and acclimatization(适应环境) of new employees—will encourage employers to shelve the idea of remote work and, like Yahoo in 2013, encourage employees to work most of the time from corporate office space.", "47": "As many office workers adapt to remote work, cities may undergo fundamental change if offices remain under-utilized. Who will benefit if working from home becomes the norm?\nEmployers argue they make considerable savings on real estate when workers shift from office to home work. However, these savings result from passing costs on to workers.\nUnless employees are fully compensated, this could become a variant of parasitic(寄生的) capitalism, whereby corporate profits increasingly rely on extracting value from the public—and now personal—realm, rather than on generating new value.\nThough employers are backed by a chorus of remote work advocates, others note the loneliness, reduced productivity and inefficiencies of extended remote work.\nIf working from home becomes permanent, employees will have to dedicate part of their private space to work. This requires purchasing desks, chairs and office equipment.\nIt also means having private space dedicated to work: the space must be heated, cleaned, maintained and paid for. That depends on many things, but for purposes of illustration, I have run some estimates for Montreal. The exercise is simple but important, since it brings these costs out of the realm of speculation into the realm of meaningful discussion.\nRough calculations show that the savings made by employers when their staff works from home are of similar value to the compensation workers should receive for setting up offices at home.\nWhat does this mean for offices in cities? One of two things may happen: Employers pass these costs onto employees. This would be a form of expropriation(侵占), with employees absorbing production costs that have traditionally been paid by the employer. This represents a considerable transfer of value from employees to employers.\nWhen employees are properly compensated, employers' real estate savings will be modest. If savings are modest, then the many advantages of working in offices—such as lively atmosphere, rapidity of communication, team-building and acclimatization(适应环境) of new employees—will encourage employers to shelve the idea of remote work and, like Yahoo in 2013, encourage employees to work most of the time from corporate office space.", "48": "As many office workers adapt to remote work, cities may undergo fundamental change if offices remain under-utilized. Who will benefit if working from home becomes the norm?\nEmployers argue they make considerable savings on real estate when workers shift from office to home work. However, these savings result from passing costs on to workers.\nUnless employees are fully compensated, this could become a variant of parasitic(寄生的) capitalism, whereby corporate profits increasingly rely on extracting value from the public—and now personal—realm, rather than on generating new value.\nThough employers are backed by a chorus of remote work advocates, others note the loneliness, reduced productivity and inefficiencies of extended remote work.\nIf working from home becomes permanent, employees will have to dedicate part of their private space to work. This requires purchasing desks, chairs and office equipment.\nIt also means having private space dedicated to work: the space must be heated, cleaned, maintained and paid for. That depends on many things, but for purposes of illustration, I have run some estimates for Montreal. The exercise is simple but important, since it brings these costs out of the realm of speculation into the realm of meaningful discussion.\nRough calculations show that the savings made by employers when their staff works from home are of similar value to the compensation workers should receive for setting up offices at home.\nWhat does this mean for offices in cities? One of two things may happen: Employers pass these costs onto employees. This would be a form of expropriation(侵占), with employees absorbing production costs that have traditionally been paid by the employer. This represents a considerable transfer of value from employees to employers.\nWhen employees are properly compensated, employers' real estate savings will be modest. If savings are modest, then the many advantages of working in offices—such as lively atmosphere, rapidity of communication, team-building and acclimatization(适应环境) of new employees—will encourage employers to shelve the idea of remote work and, like Yahoo in 2013, encourage employees to work most of the time from corporate office space.", "49": "As many office workers adapt to remote work, cities may undergo fundamental change if offices remain under-utilized. Who will benefit if working from home becomes the norm?\nEmployers argue they make considerable savings on real estate when workers shift from office to home work. However, these savings result from passing costs on to workers.\nUnless employees are fully compensated, this could become a variant of parasitic(寄生的) capitalism, whereby corporate profits increasingly rely on extracting value from the public—and now personal—realm, rather than on generating new value.\nThough employers are backed by a chorus of remote work advocates, others note the loneliness, reduced productivity and inefficiencies of extended remote work.\nIf working from home becomes permanent, employees will have to dedicate part of their private space to work. This requires purchasing desks, chairs and office equipment.\nIt also means having private space dedicated to work: the space must be heated, cleaned, maintained and paid for. That depends on many things, but for purposes of illustration, I have run some estimates for Montreal. The exercise is simple but important, since it brings these costs out of the realm of speculation into the realm of meaningful discussion.\nRough calculations show that the savings made by employers when their staff works from home are of similar value to the compensation workers should receive for setting up offices at home.\nWhat does this mean for offices in cities? One of two things may happen: Employers pass these costs onto employees. This would be a form of expropriation(侵占), with employees absorbing production costs that have traditionally been paid by the employer. This represents a considerable transfer of value from employees to employers.\nWhen employees are properly compensated, employers' real estate savings will be modest. If savings are modest, then the many advantages of working in offices—such as lively atmosphere, rapidity of communication, team-building and acclimatization(适应环境) of new employees—will encourage employers to shelve the idea of remote work and, like Yahoo in 2013, encourage employees to work most of the time from corporate office space.", "50": "As many office workers adapt to remote work, cities may undergo fundamental change if offices remain under-utilized. Who will benefit if working from home becomes the norm?\nEmployers argue they make considerable savings on real estate when workers shift from office to home work. However, these savings result from passing costs on to workers.\nUnless employees are fully compensated, this could become a variant of parasitic(寄生的) capitalism, whereby corporate profits increasingly rely on extracting value from the public—and now personal—realm, rather than on generating new value.\nThough employers are backed by a chorus of remote work advocates, others note the loneliness, reduced productivity and inefficiencies of extended remote work.\nIf working from home becomes permanent, employees will have to dedicate part of their private space to work. This requires purchasing desks, chairs and office equipment.\nIt also means having private space dedicated to work: the space must be heated, cleaned, maintained and paid for. That depends on many things, but for purposes of illustration, I have run some estimates for Montreal. The exercise is simple but important, since it brings these costs out of the realm of speculation into the realm of meaningful discussion.\nRough calculations show that the savings made by employers when their staff works from home are of similar value to the compensation workers should receive for setting up offices at home.\nWhat does this mean for offices in cities? One of two things may happen: Employers pass these costs onto employees. This would be a form of expropriation(侵占), with employees absorbing production costs that have traditionally been paid by the employer. This represents a considerable transfer of value from employees to employers.\nWhen employees are properly compensated, employers' real estate savings will be modest. If savings are modest, then the many advantages of working in offices—such as lively atmosphere, rapidity of communication, team-building and acclimatization(适应环境) of new employees—will encourage employers to shelve the idea of remote work and, like Yahoo in 2013, encourage employees to work most of the time from corporate office space.", "51": "The human thirst for knowledge is the driving force behind our successful development as a species. But curiosity can also be dangerous, leading to setbacks or even downfalls. Given curiosity's complexity, scientists have found it hard to define.\nWhile pinning down a definition has proven tricky, the general consensus is it's some means of information gathering. Psychologists also agree curiosity is intrinsically(内在地) motivated.\nCuriosity covers such a large set of behaviors that there probably isn't any single \"curiosity gene\" that makes humans wonder about and explore their environment. That said, curiosity does have a genetic component. Genes and the environment interact in many complex ways to shape individuals and guide their behavior, including their curiosity.\nRegardless of their genetic makeup, infants have to learn an incredible amount of information in a short time, and curiosity is one of the tools humans have found to accomplish that gigantic task.\nHundreds of studies show that infants prefer novelty. It's what motivates non-human animals, human infants and probably human adults to explore and seek out new things before growing less interested in them after continued exposure.\nBut curiosity often comes with a cost.\nIn some situations, the stakes are low and failure is a healthy part of growth. For instance, many babies are perfectly proficient crawlers, but they decide to try walking because there's more to see and do when they stand upright. But this milestone comes at a small cost. A study of 12- to 19-month-olds learning how to walk documented that these children fell down a lot. Seventeen times per hour, to be exact. But walking is faster than crawling, so this motivates expert crawlers to transition to walking.\nSometimes, however, testing out a new idea can lead to disaster. For instance, the Inuit people of the Arctic regions have created incredible modes to deal with the challenges of living in northern climates, but what we forget about are the tens of thousands of people that tried and failed to make it in those challenging landscapes.", "52": "The human thirst for knowledge is the driving force behind our successful development as a species. But curiosity can also be dangerous, leading to setbacks or even downfalls. Given curiosity's complexity, scientists have found it hard to define.\nWhile pinning down a definition has proven tricky, the general consensus is it's some means of information gathering. Psychologists also agree curiosity is intrinsically(内在地) motivated.\nCuriosity covers such a large set of behaviors that there probably isn't any single \"curiosity gene\" that makes humans wonder about and explore their environment. That said, curiosity does have a genetic component. Genes and the environment interact in many complex ways to shape individuals and guide their behavior, including their curiosity.\nRegardless of their genetic makeup, infants have to learn an incredible amount of information in a short time, and curiosity is one of the tools humans have found to accomplish that gigantic task.\nHundreds of studies show that infants prefer novelty. It's what motivates non-human animals, human infants and probably human adults to explore and seek out new things before growing less interested in them after continued exposure.\nBut curiosity often comes with a cost.\nIn some situations, the stakes are low and failure is a healthy part of growth. For instance, many babies are perfectly proficient crawlers, but they decide to try walking because there's more to see and do when they stand upright. But this milestone comes at a small cost. A study of 12- to 19-month-olds learning how to walk documented that these children fell down a lot. Seventeen times per hour, to be exact. But walking is faster than crawling, so this motivates expert crawlers to transition to walking.\nSometimes, however, testing out a new idea can lead to disaster. For instance, the Inuit people of the Arctic regions have created incredible modes to deal with the challenges of living in northern climates, but what we forget about are the tens of thousands of people that tried and failed to make it in those challenging landscapes.", "53": "The human thirst for knowledge is the driving force behind our successful development as a species. But curiosity can also be dangerous, leading to setbacks or even downfalls. Given curiosity's complexity, scientists have found it hard to define.\nWhile pinning down a definition has proven tricky, the general consensus is it's some means of information gathering. Psychologists also agree curiosity is intrinsically(内在地) motivated.\nCuriosity covers such a large set of behaviors that there probably isn't any single \"curiosity gene\" that makes humans wonder about and explore their environment. That said, curiosity does have a genetic component. Genes and the environment interact in many complex ways to shape individuals and guide their behavior, including their curiosity.\nRegardless of their genetic makeup, infants have to learn an incredible amount of information in a short time, and curiosity is one of the tools humans have found to accomplish that gigantic task.\nHundreds of studies show that infants prefer novelty. It's what motivates non-human animals, human infants and probably human adults to explore and seek out new things before growing less interested in them after continued exposure.\nBut curiosity often comes with a cost.\nIn some situations, the stakes are low and failure is a healthy part of growth. For instance, many babies are perfectly proficient crawlers, but they decide to try walking because there's more to see and do when they stand upright. But this milestone comes at a small cost. A study of 12- to 19-month-olds learning how to walk documented that these children fell down a lot. Seventeen times per hour, to be exact. But walking is faster than crawling, so this motivates expert crawlers to transition to walking.\nSometimes, however, testing out a new idea can lead to disaster. For instance, the Inuit people of the Arctic regions have created incredible modes to deal with the challenges of living in northern climates, but what we forget about are the tens of thousands of people that tried and failed to make it in those challenging landscapes.", "54": "The human thirst for knowledge is the driving force behind our successful development as a species. But curiosity can also be dangerous, leading to setbacks or even downfalls. Given curiosity's complexity, scientists have found it hard to define.\nWhile pinning down a definition has proven tricky, the general consensus is it's some means of information gathering. Psychologists also agree curiosity is intrinsically(内在地) motivated.\nCuriosity covers such a large set of behaviors that there probably isn't any single \"curiosity gene\" that makes humans wonder about and explore their environment. That said, curiosity does have a genetic component. Genes and the environment interact in many complex ways to shape individuals and guide their behavior, including their curiosity.\nRegardless of their genetic makeup, infants have to learn an incredible amount of information in a short time, and curiosity is one of the tools humans have found to accomplish that gigantic task.\nHundreds of studies show that infants prefer novelty. It's what motivates non-human animals, human infants and probably human adults to explore and seek out new things before growing less interested in them after continued exposure.\nBut curiosity often comes with a cost.\nIn some situations, the stakes are low and failure is a healthy part of growth. For instance, many babies are perfectly proficient crawlers, but they decide to try walking because there's more to see and do when they stand upright. But this milestone comes at a small cost. A study of 12- to 19-month-olds learning how to walk documented that these children fell down a lot. Seventeen times per hour, to be exact. But walking is faster than crawling, so this motivates expert crawlers to transition to walking.\nSometimes, however, testing out a new idea can lead to disaster. For instance, the Inuit people of the Arctic regions have created incredible modes to deal with the challenges of living in northern climates, but what we forget about are the tens of thousands of people that tried and failed to make it in those challenging landscapes.", "55": "The human thirst for knowledge is the driving force behind our successful development as a species. But curiosity can also be dangerous, leading to setbacks or even downfalls. Given curiosity's complexity, scientists have found it hard to define.\nWhile pinning down a definition has proven tricky, the general consensus is it's some means of information gathering. Psychologists also agree curiosity is intrinsically(内在地) motivated.\nCuriosity covers such a large set of behaviors that there probably isn't any single \"curiosity gene\" that makes humans wonder about and explore their environment. That said, curiosity does have a genetic component. Genes and the environment interact in many complex ways to shape individuals and guide their behavior, including their curiosity.\nRegardless of their genetic makeup, infants have to learn an incredible amount of information in a short time, and curiosity is one of the tools humans have found to accomplish that gigantic task.\nHundreds of studies show that infants prefer novelty. It's what motivates non-human animals, human infants and probably human adults to explore and seek out new things before growing less interested in them after continued exposure.\nBut curiosity often comes with a cost.\nIn some situations, the stakes are low and failure is a healthy part of growth. For instance, many babies are perfectly proficient crawlers, but they decide to try walking because there's more to see and do when they stand upright. But this milestone comes at a small cost. A study of 12- to 19-month-olds learning how to walk documented that these children fell down a lot. Seventeen times per hour, to be exact. But walking is faster than crawling, so this motivates expert crawlers to transition to walking.\nSometimes, however, testing out a new idea can lead to disaster. For instance, the Inuit people of the Arctic regions have created incredible modes to deal with the challenges of living in northern climates, but what we forget about are the tens of thousands of people that tried and failed to make it in those challenging landscapes."}, "questions": [{"id": "2021-12-1-l-1", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 1, "stem": "What does the news report say about United Airlines?", "options": ["A) It found a pet dog on board a plane to a city in Texas.", "B) It had one of its cargo planes land at a wrong airport.", "C) It sent two dogs to the wrong destinations.", "D) It had two of its domestic flights mixed up."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） It sent two dogs to the wrong destinations.\n【定位】Both of the dogs had been sent to the wrong destinations on connecting flights from Denver,\nwith Buddy mistakenly being sent to Japan instead.\n两只狗都在从丹佛转机时被送错了目的地，其中 Buddy 被错送到了日本。\n【信号】Both…wrong destinations 直陈事故。这句以 Both of the dogs had been sent to the\nwrong destinations 正面点出事故核心——两只狗都被送错目的地，是整则新闻交代『航\n空公司做了什么』的事实落点；句中 Both 与 wrong destinations 是直接承载答案的信\n号词。\n【替换】Both of the dogs had been sent to the wrong destinations ↔\nIt sent two dogs to the wrong destinations.\nBoth the dogs = two dogs\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\n选项盗用了原文里 Texas 这个地名（their new home city in Texas），但原文是说一家人\n本应在得州的新家与狗团聚，并非『在飞往得州的飞机上发现一只狗』；它把真实情节拼凑\n改写，属听到 Texas 就想选的陷阱。\n借用原文：They were meant to be reunited with the pet in their new home city in Texas.\nB) 偷换概念\n原文反复说送错的是 dog（shipping an American family's pet dog…in the wrong\ndirection），是『狗被运错方向』，从没说『一架货机降错机场』；选项把错送对象从狗偷\n换成 cargo plane、把『送错目的地』偷换成『降错机场』，与原文不符。\n借用原文：United Airlines has apologised for mistakenly shipping an American family's pet dog\nthousands of miles in the wrong direction\nD) 答非所问\n原文是两只狗在转机时被送错（on connecting flights from Denver），出错的是狗的去\n向；选项却说是『两个国内航班被搞混』，把出错对象从狗换成 flights，且原文只字未提\n『国内航班搞混』，属答非所问。\n借用原文：Both of the dogs had been sent to the wrong destinations on connecting flights\nfrom Denver", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2021-12-1-l-2", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 2, "stem": "What did the spokesperson for United Airlines say they would do after the incident?", "options": ["A) Correct their mistake as soon as possible.", "B) Give the two pets a physical checkup.", "C) Hire a charter jet to bring the pets back.", "D) Send another plane to continue the flight."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） Correct their mistake as soon as possible.\n【定位】We will arrange to return the pets to them as soon as possible.\n我们会尽快安排把宠物送还给他们。\n【信号】We will 未来承诺。这句出自发言人原话，以 We will arrange to return the pets to\nthem as soon as possible 的 We will 领头，是典型的官方补救承诺句；will 这个未来时信\n号正对应题干 say they would do，故为答案句。\n【替换】We will arrange to return the pets to them as soon as possible ↔\nCorrect their mistake as soon as possible.\n送还宠物=纠正错误\n【排除】B) 原词陷阱\n选项盗用了原文真出现的 physical checkup（Buddy was given a physical checkup），\n但那是 Buddy 到东京后已经做过的事，不是发言人承诺『事故后将要做』的动作；时间错\n位，张冠李戴到发言人头上。\n借用原文：Buddy was given a physical checkup when he arrived at Tokyo's International Airport.\nC) 原词陷阱\n选项盗用原文的 charter jet（sent back to the U.S. on a private charter jet），但那是叙\n述 Buddy 已经被私人包机送回美国的既成事实，不是发言人对今后的承诺；选项把已发生\n说成将要做，且 Hire（雇用）也非原文所述。\n借用原文：The dog was then sent back to the U.S. on a private charter jet.\nD) 未提及\n原文从没说要『再派一架飞机继续这趟飞行』；发言人只承诺尽快把宠物送还（return the\npets to them as soon as possible）。选项 Send another plane to continue the flight.\n是凭 plane/flight 这类话题词臆造的动作，未提及。\nQuestions 3 and 4 are based on the news report you have just heard.\n1 Officials at Reid Park Zoo in Tucson Arizona are celebrating the birth of a baby elephant. Zoo\nofficials say the baby was born on Monday. It is a female and she weighs nearly 130 kilograms.\n亚利桑那州图森市Reid Park动物园的官员们正在庆祝一头小象的诞生。动物园官员表示,这头小象于周一\n出生。它是雌性,体重近130公斤。\n2 Semba, her mother, is a 30-year-old African elephant. The pregnancy lasted 22 months.\n它的母亲Semba是一头30岁的非洲象。怀孕期持续了22个月。\n3 Officials describe the baby elephant as \"healthy, standing and nursing\".\n官员们描述这头小象\"健康、能站立并在哺乳\"。\n4 The baby hasn't been given a name yet.\n这头小象还没有被命名。\n5 Semba has given birth before. Zoo officials said during this pregnancy, she was closely monitored\nthrough physical exams and blood tests.\nSemba之前曾生育过。动物园官员表示,在这次怀孕期间,通过身体检查和血液测试对她进行了密切监测。\n6 Dr. Sue Tygielski is the zoo's director of zoological operations. She said the elephant's birth went\nsmoothly and that there were no complications.\nSue Tygielski博士是该动物园的动物运营总监。她表示大象的分娩过程顺利,没有出现并发症。\n7 The new baby expands the zoo's elephant herd to six. That includes the baby's parents, a sister, a\nbrother and an adult female.\n这头新生小象使动物园的大象群扩大到六头。其中包括小象的父母、一个姐姐、一个哥哥和一头成年\n雌象。", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2021-12-1-l-3", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 3, "stem": "What do we learn about the mother elephant Semba?", "options": ["A) She weighs 130 kilograms.", "B) She has had babies before.", "C) She was brought from Africa.", "D) She has a big family of six."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） She has had babies before.\n【定位】Semba has given birth before.\nSemba 以前生过孩子（不是第一次生产）。\n【信号】直陈母象过往经历。答案句是录音里专门交代 Semba 经历的一句 Semba has given birth\nbefore.，它直接陈述了关于这只母象本身的一项事实，与题干 \"关于 Semba 我们了解到什\n么\" 严丝合缝，是无需转折提示、直接点出的事实句。\n【替换】has given birth before ↔ has had babies before\n生过=有过孩子\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\n选项 She weighs 130 kilograms. 盗用了录音里真实出现的数字 130 公斤，但录音说的是\n新生小象 she weighs nearly 130 kilograms，主语是 baby 而非母亲 Semba，把宝宝的体\n重安到了母亲头上，对象搞错。\n借用原文：she weighs nearly 130 kilograms\nC) 偷换概念\n选项 She was brought from Africa. 借用了录音里的 Africa 一词，但录音只说 Semba,\nher mother, is a 30-year-old African elephant.——她是一头 \"非洲象\"（品种），并没有\n说 \"她是从非洲被带来的\"，把物种来源偷换成了运输来历。\n借用原文：a 30-year-old African elephant\nD) 原词陷阱\n选项 She has a big family of six. 盗用了录音末尾的数字 six，但录音说 The new baby\nexpands the zoo's elephant herd to six.——是 \"动物园象群扩大到六只\"，描述的是整个\n园区象群规模，而非 \"母亲 Semba 有一个六口之家\"，对象与口径都被改了。\n借用原文：expands the zoo's elephant herd to six", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2021-12-1-l-4", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 4, "stem": "What does the report say about the birth of the new baby elephant?", "options": ["A) It took 22 hours.", "B) It had some complications.", "C) It was smooth.", "D) It was monitored by Dr. Sue Tygielski."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） It was smooth.\n【定位】She said the elephant's birth went smoothly and that there were no complications.\n她（动物园动物运营主管 Sue Tygielski 博士）说，这次大象的出生过程很顺利，没有出现\n任何并发症。\n【信号】对出生过程的直接定性。答案句 the elephant's birth went smoothly and that there\nwere no complications 是录音里唯一对 \"出生过程怎么样\" 下定性的句子，went\nsmoothly 是正面评价词、no complications 是配套补充，直接回答了题干 \"出生说了什\n么\"，靠这个 \"定性词\" 信号即可锁定。\n【替换】went smoothly ↔ It was smooth.\n副词转形容词同义\nthere were no complications ↔ It was smooth.\n无并发症佐证顺利\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\n选项 It took 22 hours. 盗用了数字 22，但录音里的 22 是 The pregnancy lasted 22\nmonths.——是怀孕 22 个 \"月\"，且说的是孕期长短不是 \"出生过程用了多久\"，单位\n（hours↔months）和对象（出生过程↔整个孕期）双双被换。\n借用原文：The pregnancy lasted 22 months\nB) 与原文相反\n选项 It had some complications. 直接盗用录音原词 complications，但录音是 there\nwere no complications——明确说 \"没有\" 并发症，选项却说 \"有一些\"，把否定改成了肯\n定，极性正好相反。\n借用原文：there were no complications\nD) 张冠李戴\n选项 It was monitored by Dr. Sue Tygielski. 把录音里真实出现的人名 Sue Tygielski 搬来\n当出生过程的监控者，但录音说被密切监控的是 during this pregnancy, she was closely\nmonitored——监控的是 Semba 的 \"孕期\"，而 Tygielski 博士只是在 Dr. Sue Tygielski is\nthe zoo's director 里被介绍头衔并转述 \"出生很顺利\"，并没说出生由她监控，把 \"孕期被\n监控\" 和 \"这位博士\" 硬拼到一起。\n借用原文：during this pregnancy, she was closely monitored", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2021-12-1-l-5", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 5, "stem": "What do we learn about the restaurant?", "options": ["A) It enjoyed great popularity.", "B) It started business three years ago.", "C) It was frequented by newly-weds.", "D) It was built above the sea-water."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） It was built above the sea-water.\n【定位】The restaurant was built on a wooden structure above the sea-water.\n这家餐厅建在海水之上的一个木质结构上。\n【信号】录音里唯一直接描述餐厅本身的句子。全篇只有这一句在正面描述餐厅这个事物本身——\nThe restaurant was built on a wooden structure above the sea-water.，其余句子讲的\n是戒指、经理、服务员的动作；当题问某事物的事实，就锁定录音里主语正是该事物、且在\n交代它属性的那句。\n【替换】built on a wooden structure above the sea-water ↔ It was built above the sea-water.\n选项是原句的压缩概括\n【排除】A) 未提及\nIt enjoyed great popularity. 说餐厅很受欢迎，但录音通篇只讲戒指失而复得，从未评价餐\n厅的人气或生意好坏，属于无中生有。\nB) 原词陷阱\nIt started business three years ago. 借了录音开头的 three years ago，但 Three years\nago, a couple was enjoying a meal 里这个时间是指三年前有对夫妇在此用餐、戒指掉\n落，根本不是讲餐厅三年前开业；把三年前从用餐时间偷换成开业时间，是典型的原词陷\n阱。\n借用原文：Three years ago, a couple was enjoying a meal at a beach restaurant.\nC) 过度推断\nIt was frequented by newly-weds. 说餐厅常被新婚夫妇光顾；录音只提到一对夫妇曾来用\n餐 a couple was enjoying a meal，既没说他们是新婚、更没说这里常有新婚夫妇来，属\n于由一对夫妇过度推断成常客群体。\n借用原文：a couple was enjoying a meal at a beach restaurant", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2021-12-1-l-6", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 6, "stem": "What did the restaurant manager decide to do recently?", "options": ["A) Expand his business on the beach.", "B) Replace the restaurant's wooden deck.", "C) Post a picture of his restaurant online.", "D) Celebrate his silver wedding anniversary."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） Replace the restaurant's wooden deck.\n【定位】Recently, the restaurant manager Ryan Krivoy decided to replace the wooden deck and he\nfound an old gold coin, some $100 bills and a silver wedding ring while replacing the deck.\n最近，餐厅经理 Ryan Krivoy 决定更换那个木质平台，他在换平台时发现了一枚古老的金\n币、一些百元钞票和一枚银质结婚戒指。\n【信号】decided to 直接给出经理的决定动作。the restaurant manager Ryan Krivoy decided to\nreplace the wooden deck 里 decided to 后面紧跟的动词短语，就是题目要的决定；听到\ndecided to 这种表决定的信号词，后面接的动作即是答案落点。\n【替换】decided to replace the wooden deck ↔ Replace the restaurant's wooden deck.\n几乎同表述，仅补出所属\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\nExpand his business on the beach. 里的 beach 借自录音背景中的海边餐厅（beach\nrestaurant），但录音从没说经理要扩张生意，他决定的是换平台 replace the wooden\ndeck；把场景词 beach 拿来拼一个原文没有的动作，是原词陷阱加无中生有。\n借用原文：a beach restaurant\nC) 张冠李戴\nPost a picture of his restaurant online. 里发照片到网上这件事，录音里做的人是女服务\n员 The restaurant waitress Sasha Formica posted a picture of the ring on\nFacebook.，且发的是戒指照片不是餐厅照片；既把动作的人从服务员张冠李戴到经理，又\n把戒指换成餐厅，双重偷换。\n借用原文：The restaurant waitress Sasha Formica posted a picture of the ring on Facebook.\nD) 原词陷阱\nCelebrate his silver wedding anniversary. 里的 silver wedding 拼自录音的 silver\nwedding ring（银质结婚戒指），但 a silver wedding ring 说的是经理找到的一枚银戒\n指，根本不是他要庆祝结婚周年；把 silver wedding ring 截成 silver wedding，是诱人的\n原词陷阱。\n借用原文：a silver wedding ring", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2021-12-1-l-7", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 7, "stem": "What did the waitress do about the recovered ring?", "options": ["A) She sold it for two thousand dollars.", "B) She took it to the restaurant manager.", "C) She posted its picture on Facebook.", "D) She returned it to its owner right away."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） She posted its picture on Facebook.\n【定位】The restaurant waitress Sasha Formica posted a picture of the ring on Facebook.\n餐厅女服务员 Sasha Formica 把戒指的照片发到了脸书上。\n【信号】主语正是 waitress、动作直接针对 ring。The restaurant waitress Sasha Formica\nposted a picture of the ring on Facebook. 这一句主语恰是题目问的 waitress、动作\nposted 又直接落在 ring 上，人、物、动作三者齐全；当题目锁定某人对某物的动作，就找\n主语是该人、宾语含该物的那一句。\n【替换】posted a picture of the ring on Facebook ↔ She posted its picture on Facebook.\n几乎原句，仅用 its 回指 ring\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\nShe sold it for two thousand dollars. 里的两千美元搬自录音结尾 the gold coin was\nvery rare. It was from 1855 and worth as much as $2,000，但这金额是说那枚稀有金币\n值两千美元、并非服务员把戒指卖了两千；既盗用了金额数字，又把对象从金币偷换成戒\n指，是原词陷阱加偷换概念。\n借用原文：It was from 1855 and worth as much as $2,000.\nB) 未提及\nShe took it to the restaurant manager. 说服务员把戒指交给经理，但录音里其实是经理\n先发现戒指 he found ... a silver wedding ring、服务员再去发照片，戒指并非由服务员上\n交经理；这是个原文没发生、还把人物关系方向弄反的干扰项。\nD) 张冠李戴\nShe returned it to its owner right away. 借了录音里确有的归还戒指情节，但还戒指的是\n餐厅而非服务员 The restaurant mailed the ring back to the happy couple.，而且是失\n主三天后来认领 Three days later, the happy wife called to claim the ring 才寄还，并非\nright away（立刻）；张冠李戴加篡改时间。\n借用原文：The restaurant mailed the ring back to the happy couple.", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2021-12-1-l-8", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 8, "stem": "What does the woman find unbelievable?", "options": ["A) The number of ducks has declined sharply in recent years.", "B) Climate change has little effect on the lives of wild ducks.", "C) Duck meat is not eaten in Australia, Canada and the U.S.", "D) Duck hunting remains legal in many parts of the world."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （态度观点题） Duck hunting remains legal in many parts of the world.\n【定位】W: I can't believe that duck hunting is still legal in so many parts of the world.\n女：我真不敢相信，在世界上这么多地方猎鸭居然还是合法的。\n【信号】“我不敢相信”直接喊出态度。答案信号是开口第一句的强烈表态 I can't believe that——\n这正是 unbelievable 的口语版，紧跟其后的内容就是她觉得难以置信的对象，无需再往\n下找。\n【替换】duck hunting is still legal in so many parts of the world ↔\nDuck hunting remains legal in many parts of the world.\nis still=remains 的同义替换\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\n录音确实提到 a decline in the birds' numbers，A 用 declined sharply 盗了这个原词。\n但她惊讶的是“居然合法”，数量下降只是她用来佐证的事实，答非所问。\n借用原文：a decline in the birds' numbers\nB) 与原文相反\n录音里 It's caused by both in fact. 明确说气候（干旱）是原因之一，B 却说 Climate\nchange has little effect，与原文相反；climate change 这个词是男士在 climate change\n提出的疑问，被借来做诱饵。\n借用原文：It's caused by both in fact.\nC) 偷换概念\n录音说 Their meat is hardly eaten in Western countries.，指“西方国家”。C 把范围偷换\n成 Australia, Canada and the U.S. 三个具体国家，且这是后文的细节、不是她开头表态的\n“不敢相信”对象。\n借用原文：Their meat is hardly eaten in Western countries.", "points": ["听力·长对话", "态度观点题"]}, {"id": "2021-12-1-l-9", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 9, "stem": "What does the woman say has caused the shrinking of ducks' habitats in Australia?", "options": ["A) Droughts.", "B) Bushfires.", "C) Farming.", "D) Hunting."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） Droughts.\n【定位】W: We see more droughts in countries like Australia. Ducks are birds that feed and breed in\nareas where there is a lot of water, but their habitats have been shrinking in recent decades\nbecause of the droughts.\n女：在澳大利亚这样的国家，我们看到了更多的干旱。鸭子是在水多的地方觅食和繁殖的\n鸟，但近几十年来，由于干旱，它们的栖息地一直在缩小。\n【信号】because of 后面直接点出原因。答案信号是明确的因果短语 because of the droughts\n——habitats shrinking 紧跟 because of，连接词后的 droughts 就是栖息地缩小的直接\n原因。\n【替换】because of the droughts ↔ Droughts.\n原文与选项同词直接对应\n【排除】B) 未提及\nBushfires. 只是“澳大利亚”这一情境下考生脑补出来的常识，录音从头到尾未提 bushfire，\n属凭印象的无中生有。\nC) 原词陷阱\n录音后面才提到 farming areas、农民毒鸭等，C 用 Farming. 盗了这个原词，但题问的是\n“栖息地缩小的原因”，农业与之并无因果关系，答非所问。\n借用原文：farming areas\nD) 原词陷阱\n录音里 hunting 与 a decline in the birds' numbers 相关，D 用 Hunting. 盗了全篇高频\n词。但题专问 habitats 缩小，原文把这一项的因明确归给 droughts，hunting 答非所问。\n借用原文：It's caused by both in fact.", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2021-12-1-l-10", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 10, "stem": "Why is there no commercial farming of ducks and geese in Western countries?", "options": ["A) They are not easy to domesticate.", "B) Their meat is not that popular.", "C) It is not environmentally friendly.", "D) It is not considered cost-effective."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） Their meat is not that popular.\n【定位】W: Yet, there's no commercial farming of these birds nowadays. Their meat is hardly eaten in\nWestern countries.\n女：然而，如今并没有对这些鸟类的商业养殖。在西方国家，它们的肉几乎没人吃。\n【信号】现象句紧跟的下一句即解释。答案信号是“现象+紧贴解释”的相邻结构：先说 there's no\ncommercial farming（现象），紧接着 Their meat is hardly eaten in Western\ncountries. 就是原因——没人吃，自然不必养。\n【替换】Their meat is hardly eaten in Western countries. ↔ Their meat is not that popular.\n“几乎没人吃”=“不太受欢迎”\n【排除】A) 未提及\nThey are not easy to domesticate. 听着像个合理理由，但录音没说过驯养难易，属凭常\n识脑补的无中生有。\nC) 未提及\nIt is not environmentally friendly. 蹭了“环保”这个当下热词，但录音解释“不养”的理由是\n肉没人吃，从未提环保，无中生有。\nD) 过度推断\nIt is not considered cost-effective. 看似能从“没人吃→养了不赚钱”推出来，但这是考生自\n己多走的一步，原文只说肉没人吃，没说成本不划算。\n借用原文：Their meat is hardly eaten in Western countries.", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2021-12-1-l-11", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 11, "stem": "What does the woman say about farmers in her grandfather's time?", "options": ["A) They hunted ducks as a traditional sport.", "B) They killed wild ducks and geese for food.", "C) They raised ducks and geese for their eggs.", "D) They poisoned wild ducks in large numbers."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） They poisoned wild ducks in large numbers.\n【定位】W: ducks have never been popular with farmers like my grandfather anyway because they\nsometimes destroy crops. In fact, farmers used to poison them in large numbers.\n女：反正鸭子从来都不受我祖父那样的农民待见，因为它们有时会毁坏庄稼。事实上，农民\n过去常常大量毒杀它们。\n【信号】主语为 farmers 的应答句直接点动作。答案信号是女士应答里主语明确为 farmers 的那句\nfarmers used to poison them in large numbers——题问 farmers，原文恰由 farmers\n当主语给出动作，定位即在此。\n【替换】farmers used to poison them in large numbers ↔ They poisoned wild ducks in large numbers.\n原话与选项近乎逐词对应\n【排除】A) 张冠李戴\nThey hunted ducks as a traditional sport. 蹭了男士那句 duck hunting seemingly\nexists as the continuation of a tradition 里的 tradition，但那是讲“如今猎鸭是传统延\n续”，主语不是祖父年代的农民，张冠李戴。\n借用原文：duck hunting seemingly exists as the continuation of a tradition\nB) 张冠李戴\nThey killed wild ducks and geese for food. 盗用了 a plentifully available food\nsource，但那讲的是祖父那辈猎人把鸭鹅当食物来源，主语是猎人而非本题问的 farmers，\n且农民对鸭子的态度是毒杀而非食用，张冠李戴。\n借用原文：a plentifully available food source\nC) 未提及\nThey raised ducks and geese for their eggs. 里的 raised（饲养）和 eggs（蛋）录音从\n未念到——原文讲的全是猎杀、毒杀，没有任何“养鸭取蛋”，属无中生有。", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2021-12-1-l-12", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 12, "stem": "What is the woman planning to do?", "options": ["A) Have her house repainted.", "B) Replace some of her old furniture.", "C) Move into a newly-painted house.", "D) Calculate the cost of the paint job."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） Have her house repainted.\n【定位】W: So, how much would the paint job cost?\n女：那么，这次刷漆要花多少钱呢？\n【信号】她默认刷漆为前提。整段对话从男方 I estimate you have between 210 and 240 square\nmeters of walls and ceiling. 量墙面起，到女方追问 how much would the paint job\ncost，全程围绕 the paint job 推进——她来咨询、谈价钱，前提就是她正打算把房子刷一\n遍。信号是「她把刷漆当作既定要做的事在谈费用」，而非在犹豫要不要刷。\n【替换】the paint job ↔ Have her house repainted.\n刷漆=请人重刷房子\n【排除】B) 原词陷阱\n录音确实念到 furniture，但只在 we can't paint the walls without clearing all the\nfurniture first 里讲「刷漆要先搬开家具」，从没说她要更换旧家具。这一项盗用 furniture\n这个原词制造「听到就想选」的诱惑，实则答非所问。\n借用原文：we can't paint the walls without clearing all the furniture first\nC) 偷换概念\n录音里女方说的是 I would have to stay with a friend or check into a hotel，即刷漆那两\n天临时去别处住，绝不是搬进一所新刷好的房子。这一项借「新刷好的房子」这一刷漆语境\n的联想来误导，与原文意思不符。\n借用原文：I would have to stay with a friend or check into a hotel\nD) 张冠李戴\n录音开头是男方说 I've finished calculating.——计算面积、费用的是这位男技师，不是女\n方；女方只是问价。这一项盗用 calculate/cost 这两个原词，又把「算钱」这个动作张冠李\n戴到女方身上。\n借用原文：I've finished calculating.", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2021-12-1-l-13", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 13, "stem": "What is the woman's chief concern?", "options": ["A) How long the work will take.", "B) How much the work will cost.", "C) How the paint job is to be done.", "D) How many workers are needed."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） How much the work will cost.\n【定位】W: So, how much would the paint job cost?\n女：那么，这次刷漆要花多少钱呢？\n【信号】她主动追问的就是钱。女方主动开口问的第一件事就是 how much would the paint job\ncost，随后听到报价 $3,000 又反应 that's more than I expected——她的注意力始终落在\n费用上。信号是「她主动发问且事后又对价钱有强烈反应」，这正标记出她的 chief\nconcern 是钱。\n【替换】how much would the paint job cost ↔ How much the work will cost.\nthe paint job=the work\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\n录音里 the whole task would take two days 确实念到两天工期，但这是男方主动告知\n的，不是女方在意发问的。这一项盗用「两天/工期」这一原词信息，却不是她的 chief\nconcern。\n借用原文：the whole task would take two days\nC) 答非所问\n施工方式由男方在 every time we paint a room, we first have to move the furniture to\nanother room 主动解释，女方只是问了句 Why?，并未把「怎么刷」当成核心关切。这一\n项借「刷漆流程」这段原文诱选，却答非所问。\n借用原文：every time we paint a room, we first have to move the furniture to another room\nD) 未提及\n录音里只在 it requires two people 提到「需要两个人」是抬高费用的原因，而不是讨论\n「需要几个工人」这个独立话题，女方更没把人数当关切。选项把 two people 偷换成「需\n要多少工人」这个题目，属未提及该问法。", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2021-12-1-l-14", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 14, "stem": "What does the woman have to do while the paint job is being done?", "options": ["A) Cover up her furniture.", "B) Ask some friends for help.", "C) Stay somewhere else.", "D) Oversee the work herself."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） Stay somewhere else.\n【定位】W: Oh well, that changes everything, I'm afraid. I would have to stay with a friend or check\ninto a hotel.\n女：哦，那这下情况就全变了，恐怕得这样。我就只能去朋友那儿住，或者住进酒店了。\n【信号】I would have to 必要性信号。前一句男方确认 That is correct.（指她那两天不能住在自\n己家），紧接着女方说 I would have to stay with a friend or check into a hotel，have\nto 正是「不得不」的必要性信号。信号是「条件触发后她亲口说出的 have to + 动作」，\n这就是答案句。\n【替换】stay with a friend or check into a hotel ↔ Stay somewhere else.\n住朋友家或酒店=去别处住\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\n录音里和家具有关的是 the price includes moving all the furniture——搬家具由施工方\n包办，根本没说要女方去「盖住」家具。这一项盗用 furniture 原词，又把动作安错了执行\n人。\n借用原文：the price includes moving all the furniture\nB) 偷换概念\n录音里 friend 出现在 stay with a friend——是去朋友家借住，不是叫朋友来帮忙刷漆。这\n一项盗用 friend 这个原词，把「投奔朋友住」偷换成「请朋友帮忙干活」。\n借用原文：stay with a friend\nD) 未提及\n录音通篇没有女方要「亲自盯着施工」的内容，反而她说自己 I would have to stay with a\nfriend or check into a hotel，两天不在家，更谈不上监工。这一项是凭刷漆场景空想出来\n的，原文无据。", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2021-12-1-l-15", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 15, "stem": "What has the woman come to realize at the end of the conversation?", "options": ["A) She could have asked a friend for help with the paint job.", "B) Painting a house involves more trouble than she thought.", "C) She should have repainted her house much earlier.", "D) Moving her furniture is harder than the paint job."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） Painting a house involves more trouble than she thought.\n【定位】W: I'm starting to realize that painting my house is far more troublesome than I\nhad anticipated.\n女：我开始意识到，给我的房子刷漆远比我原先料想的要麻烦得多。\n【信号】realize 引出的结尾总结。这句出现在对话接近尾声处，由 I'm starting to realize 领起，正\n是她亲口说出的醒悟；后文男方还附和 Most of our clients go through the same\nrealization. 加以印证。信号是「结尾处由 realize 引出、且对方附和的总结句」，这就是答\n案句。\n【替换】painting my house is far more troublesome than I had anticipated ↔\nPainting a house involves more trouble than she thought.\nmore troublesome=more trouble\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\n录音里 friend 只在 stay with a friend（去朋友家借住）出现，从没说「本可以找朋友帮忙\n刷漆」。这一项盗用 friend 原词，又用 could have 这个虚拟句式凭空造了个她没说过的懊\n悔。\n借用原文：stay with a friend\nC) 未提及\n对话里没有任何「她后悔没早点刷房子」的内容，她的醒悟是刷漆太麻烦，与时间早晚无\n关。这一项用 should have repainted 的虚拟语气编了一个原文不存在的后悔。\nD) 偷换概念\n录音里搬家具是 the price includes moving all the furniture 由公司负责的一个环节，女\n方并没有说「搬家具比刷漆本身还难」。这一项盗用 furniture/moving 原词，把她「整件\n事很麻烦」的醒悟偷换成「搬家具比刷漆难」这个原文没下过的比较。\n借用原文：the price includes moving all the furniture", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2021-12-1-l-16", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 16, "stem": "What is the main aim of homework for primary school kids?", "options": ["A) To cultivate good habits.", "B) To prepare for secondary school.", "C) To review what is learned in class.", "D) To stimulate interest in learning."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） To cultivate good habits.\n【定位】At the primary level, the main aim is to cultivate good habits, like learning to plan and\nexercising self-discipline.\n在小学阶段，主要目的是培养好习惯，比如学会做计划和锻炼自律。\n【信号】学段词+main aim原话直给。答案句以 At the primary level 点出学段，紧跟 the main\naim is to cultivate good habits 几乎把题干的 main aim 原样念出——这种『题干关键词\n在录音里被复述』的句子就是答案句信号。\n【替换】the main aim is to cultivate good habits ↔ To cultivate good habits.\n近乎原词对应\n【排除】B) 张冠李戴\nTo prepare for secondary school. 借了录音里 During the secondary school years 的\nsecondary school 一词，但录音讲 secondary 时说的是『延伸课堂所学』，根本没说小学\n是为升中学做准备——把中学段的话错安到了小学目的上。\n借用原文：During the secondary school years\nC) 答非所问\nTo review what is learned in class. 像是改写自录音 extending what is learned at\nschool，但那句讲的是『中学阶段』延伸课堂所学，并非小学目的；且 review（复习）也\n不等于 extending（延伸/拓展）。\n借用原文：extending what is learned at school\nD) 未提及\nTo stimulate interest in learning. 听起来很合理（作业激发学习兴趣），但录音讲小学目\n的只说了 cultivate good habits、learning to plan、self-discipline，全程没提『激发兴\n趣』，属于凭常识脑补出来的选项。", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2021-12-1-l-17", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 17, "stem": "What does the passage suggest parents do to help their children?", "options": ["A) Discuss their academic achievements with them.", "B) Create an ideal study environment for them.", "C) Allow them to learn independently.", "D) Check their homework promptly."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） Create an ideal study environment for them.\n【定位】Creating an ideal working environment will make it easier for them to get down to their\nassignments quickly.\n营造一个理想的学习环境，会让孩子更容易快速投入到作业中。\n【信号】首句建议句直陈做法。第 3 段开头 Creating an ideal working environment 就是一条建\n议家长落地去做的具体动作，篇章里这种『动名词/祈使开头、紧跟好处』的句子是典型建\n议落点。\n【替换】Creating an ideal working environment ↔ Create an ideal study environment for them.\nworking≈study的同义替换\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\nDiscuss their academic achievements with them. 借了录音 academic achievement 这\n个原词，但那是在讲『中学作业与学业成绩正相关』，根本没让家长去和孩子『讨论成绩』\n——听到 achievement 就选正中陷阱。\n借用原文：academic achievement\nC) 与原文相反\nAllow them to learn independently. 看似呼应录音 by the end of primary school it's\nsecond nature（小学末要变成自觉），但录音始终在讲家长要『帮忙、陪在旁边』\n（somewhere near you），方向是陪伴而非放手，选独立自学正好反了家长此刻该做的\n事。\n借用原文：somewhere near you\nD) 原词陷阱\nCheck their homework promptly. 用了话题原词 homework，特别勾人，但录音从没让家\n长『及时检查作业』，只说营造环境、陪在旁边答疑鼓励（answer questions and offer\nencouragement），check homework 是无中生有。\n借用原文：answer questions and offer encouragement", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2021-12-1-l-18", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 18, "stem": "What should children do to deal with multiple assignments?", "options": ["A) Finish them before they get tired.", "B) Tackle the most difficult task first.", "C) Start with something they enjoy.", "D) Focus on the most important ones."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） Start with something they enjoy.\n【定位】When there are several different assignments, make sure they begin with one they enjoy, so it\nseems easy to get started.\n当有好几项不同的作业时，要确保孩子从一项他们喜欢的开始做，这样起步就显得轻松。\n【信号】情境词+begin with点起手。录音先用 When there are several different assignments 复\n述了题干的『多项作业』情境，紧跟 begin with one they enjoy 用次序词 begin with 直\n接点出第一步——情境复述+次序词就是答案句信号。\n【替换】begin with one they enjoy ↔ Start with something they enjoy.\nbegin with=Start with的同义替换\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\nFinish them before they get tired. 借了录音 before they get tired 的 tired 一词，但原\n文 before they get tired 是修饰『第二步做最难的要赶在累之前』，不是说『趁不累把全\n部做完』——把限定条件偷换成了总目标。\n借用原文：before they get tired\nB) 与原文相反\nTackle the most difficult task first. 用了录音原词 the most difficult task，极其诱人，但\n录音说的是 take on the most difficult task second——是『第二步』才做最难的，选项\n却改成 first，次序被反，与原文相反。\n借用原文：take on the most difficult task second\nD) 未提及\nFocus on the most important ones. 的 most important 听着和 18B 的 most difficult\n很像、也很合常理，但录音排序依据是『喜欢的先、最难的第二』，从没按『重要性』排，\nimportant 是脑补出来的。", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2021-12-1-l-19", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 19, "stem": "Who will qualify for the bonus in Mexican oil company?", "options": ["A) Workers who meet its body weight standards.", "B) Workers who can lose 30 pounds in a year.", "C) Workers who try the hardest to lose weight.", "D) Workers who are in the top 10% of the slimmest."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） Workers who meet its body weight standards.\n【定位】Workers at Mexican oil company will receive a healthy incentive of almost $300 a year if\nthey meet certain body weight standards. To qualify for the bonus, they must maintain a\nhealthy weight.\n墨西哥这家石油公司的员工，如果达到一定的体重标准，每年可领到将近 300 美元的健康奖\n励。要拿到这笔奖金，他们必须保持健康的体重。\n【信号】qualify 与 if 引出的资格条件句。录音出现题干同源词 qualify，且紧跟 To qualify for the\nbonus, they must maintain a healthy weight 这一明确的资格条件句，加上前句 if they\nmeet certain body weight standards 的 if 条件，正是承载答案的信号句。\n【替换】they meet certain body weight standards ↔ meet its body weight standards\n近乎同表，certain→its 的限定替换\nthey must maintain a healthy weight ↔ meet its body weight standards\n保持健康体重=达到体重标准\n【排除】B) 原词陷阱\nB 借了录音里的减重情节，但录音说的是 reduce their weight by 10%（减重 10%），选\n项却写成 lose 30 pounds in a year（一年减 30 磅），数字被偷换，属听到减重就想选的\n原词陷阱。\n借用原文：reduce their weight by 10% during the year\nC) 未提及\nC 说奖给 Workers who try the hardest to lose weight（最努力减重的人），录音判定标\n准只看体重结果（达标或减 10%），从没提以努力程度评奖，属无中生有。\nD) 原词陷阱\nD 用了录音里的数字 10%，但录音的 reduce their weight by 10% 是减重幅度 10%，选\n项却说成 in the top 10% of the slimmest（最瘦的前 10%），把幅度偷换成排名，属原\n词陷阱。\n借用原文：reduce their weight by 10% during the year", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2021-12-1-l-20", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 20, "stem": "What do critics think of Mexican oil company's bonus policy?", "options": ["A) Impractical.", "B) Inconsistent.", "C) Unmanageable.", "D) Unfair."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （态度观点题） Unfair.\n【定位】But critics say the policy contradicts recommendations by many health experts, who warn\nthat a person's weight is influenced by genetic and environmental factors. That means weight\ncan be hard for individuals to control, which makes the bonus unfair.\n但批评者说，这项政策与许多健康专家的建议相抵触，这些专家警告说，一个人的体重受遗\n传和环境因素影响。这意味着体重对个人来说可能难以控制，这就使得这笔奖金不公平。\n【信号】题干主语 critics 出现 + 转折 But + 结论 unfair。录音以 But critics say 直接对上题干的\ncritics，转折词 But 标示真正的批评态度要来了，段末 which makes the bonus unfair 给\n出批评者的定性结论，是答案句。\n【替换】which makes the bonus unfair ↔ Unfair\n原词直现的评价结论\n【排除】A) 未提及\nA 说政策 Impractical（不切实际），录音批评者的定性是 unfair（不公平），从未说它不\n切实际，属未提及的近义干扰。\nB) 张冠李戴\nB 的 Inconsistent 确是录音原词，但说它的是第 4 段的 Some experts say the policy is\ninconsistent，而本题问的是 critics（批评者）的看法，二者不是同一方，属张冠李戴。\n借用原文：Some experts say the policy is inconsistent with the science\nC) 偷换概念\nC 说政策 Unmanageable（难以管理），录音里批评者谈的是体重难以由个人控制（hard\nfor individuals to control），讲的是个人控体重难，不是政策难管理，属偷换概念加未提\n及。", "points": ["听力·讲座", "态度观点题"]}, {"id": "2021-12-1-l-21", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 21, "stem": "What do experts propose companies do about workers' wellbeing?", "options": ["A) Offer them much fatter bonuses.", "B) Improve working environment.", "C) Encourage healthy behaviors.", "D) Provide free lunch and snacks."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） Encourage healthy behaviors.\n【定位】These experts argue that focusing on wage is the wrong approach. A more productive method\nof encouraging health among employees is to promote healthy behaviors.\n这些专家认为，盯着工资是错误的做法。在员工中促进健康的一种更有效的方法，是推动健\n康的行为。\n【信号】对比 wrong approach 与 A more productive method 引出的建议句。录音先用\nfocusing on wage is the wrong approach 否定旧法，紧接 A more productive\nmethod… is to promote healthy behaviors 这个更有效的方法句正面给出专家建议，是\n承载答案的信号句。\n【替换】to promote healthy behaviors ↔ Encourage healthy behaviors\npromote=encourage 的近义替换\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\nA 说 Offer them much fatter bonuses（发更肥的奖金），借了全篇核心词 bonus，但专\n家明说 focusing on wage is the wrong approach，盯着钱正是被否定的错误做法，属原\n词陷阱加与原文相反。\n借用原文：focusing on wage is the wrong approach\nB) 未提及\nB 说 Improve working environment，录音专家给的建议是促进健康行为并举了健身、讲\n座、健康饮食等例，没提改善工作环境这一概括，属未提及。\nD) 答非所问\nD 的 Provide free lunch and snacks 确与录音 provide healthier snacks… and healthier\nmeals at the company canteen 沾边，但那只是促进健康行为的具体举例之一，题目要的\n是上位的建议，拿例子冒充结论，属以偏概全。\n借用原文：provide healthier snacks in the office and healthier meals at the company canteen", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2021-12-1-l-22", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 22, "stem": "Why are some people criticizing the British government?", "options": ["A) It has not done enough to help left-handed children.", "B) It has treated left-handed children as being disabled.", "C) It has not built facilities specially for the left-handed.", "D) It has ignored campaigns on behalf of the left-handed."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） It has not done enough to help left-handed children.\n【定位】Campaigners have warned that the British government is not doing enough to prevent left-\nhanded pupils from falling behind their peers.\n活动倡导者警告说，英国政府在防止左撇子学生落后于同龄人方面做得不够。\n【信号】warned 引出的批评首句。录音开篇即由 Campaigners have warned that 引出批评，\nwarned(警告)是明确的指责信号，紧随其后的 the British government is not doing\nenough 就是被批评的具体内容，是承载答案的那一句。\n【替换】the British government is not doing enough to prevent left-handed pupils from falling behind ↔\nIt has not done enough to help left-handed children\n防止落后=帮助，措辞改写\n【排除】B) 未提及\ntreated left-handed children as being disabled 说政府\"把左撇子当残疾人对待\"，录音从\n头到尾没有 disabled 这个说法，是凭\"左撇子常被特殊对待\"的常识硬推出来的，属未提\n及。\nC) 偷换概念\nnot built facilities specially for the left-handed 把批评落到\"没建专门设施\"。录音批评的\n是政府/部长 failed to take any meaningful action\\(没采取有意义的行动\\)，从未提\"设\n施\"；选项用一个看似具体的硬件方向偷换了\"行动不足\"这一真正指责点。\nD) 张冠李戴\nignored campaigns on behalf of the left-handed 说政府\"无视了为左撇子发起的呼吁\"。\n录音里 Campaigners(活动倡导者)是发出批评的一方，Campaigners have warned；选项\n盗用了 campaign 这个原词，却把\"倡导者在批评\"反说成\"政府无视呼吁\"，是把发声方和被\n批方混在一起的张冠李戴，且\"无视呼吁\"不等于首句明指的\"做得不够\"。\n借用原文：Campaigners have warned that the British government is not doing enough", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2021-12-1-l-23", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 23, "stem": "What do studies in recent years show about left-handed children?", "options": ["A) They are as intelligent as other children.", "B) They have a distinctive style of handwriting.", "C) They sometimes have psychological problems.", "D) They tend to have more difficulties in learning."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） They tend to have more difficulties in learning.\n【定位】Studies in recent years show that left-handed children are more likely to suffer with learning\ndifficulties and their scores are lower on IQ tests.\n近年的研究表明，左撇子孩子更可能存在学习困难，他们在智商测试中的得分也更低。\n【信号】Studies show 引出的结论句。录音 Studies in recent years show that 与题干用词几乎重\n合，是明显的定位信号；其后 more likely to suffer with learning difficulties 就是研究给\n出的结论，承载答案。\n【替换】more likely to suffer with learning difficulties ↔ tend to have more difficulties in learning\n更可能有=更倾向有，近义改写\n【排除】A) 与原文相反\nas intelligent as other children\\(和别人一样聪明\\)与录音相反：录音说 their scores are\nlower on IQ tests\\(智商测试得分更低\\)，恰恰是\"不一样/更低\"，选项把极性反了过来。\n借用原文：their scores are lower on IQ tests\nB) 原词陷阱\na distinctive style of handwriting\\(独特的书写风格\\)盗用了录音多处提到的\nhandwriting。录音里 handwriting 是 poor handwriting\\(糟糕的书写\\)、是困难，绝\n非\"独特风格\"这种中性/褒义说法；本题问的又是 studies 的结论，与书写无关，属答非所\n问。\n借用原文：poor handwriting\nC) 未提及\npsychological problems\\(心理问题\\)在 studies 那句里没有对应，录音 P3 只说了\nlearning difficulties 和 IQ 得分低，没提心理；\"左撇子有心理问题\"是听起来合理的常识联\n想，但录音没念到。", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2021-12-1-l-24", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 24, "stem": "What do the campaigners demand the department of education do?", "options": ["A) Punish teachers discriminating against left-handed students.", "B) Lay more emphasis on improving children's mental health.", "C) Encourage students to develop various professional skills.", "D) Keep track of left-handed children's school performance."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） Keep track of left-handed children's school performance.\n【定位】They want the department of education to record which children are left-handed and what\ntheir educational attainments are.\n他们希望教育部门记录哪些孩子是左撇子、以及他们的学业成绩如何。\n【信号】want 引出的诉求句。录音 They want the department of education to 用 want 直接给\n出诉求，want 后面接的 record which children are left-handed and what their\neducational attainments are 就是要求做的事，是答案句。\n【替换】record which children are left-handed and what their educational attainments are ↔\nKeep track of left-handed children's school performance\n记录学业成绩=追踪学业表现\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\nPunish teachers discriminating against left-handed students 借了录音末段的\nteacher(出现在 a lack of teacher training\\)。录音说的是\"缺乏教师培训\"，从没要求\"惩罚\n老师\"；Punish 这一强硬动作是录音没有的，属原词陷阱。\n借用原文：a lack of teacher training\nB) 未提及\nimproving children's mental health 把诉求引向\"心理健康\"。录音的诉求只在\"记录左撇子\n身份与学业成绩\"，全篇没提 mental health；本项用一个貌似教育部门该管的方向偷换了真\n正的诉求。\nC) 答非所问\ndevelop various professional skills\\(发展各种职业技能\\)在录音诉求句里没有对应。录音\nP2 虽提到 reduced career prospects\\(职业前景受损\\)，但那是描述后果，不是\"要求教育\n部门去发展技能\"，本项答非所问。\n借用原文：facing reduced career prospects", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2021-12-1-l-25", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 25, "stem": "What do the campaigners want to know about left-handed prisoners?", "options": ["A) How they can be reduced in number.", "B) Why their numbers are so high.", "C) What percentage they account for.", "D) If their percentage keeps increasing."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） Why their numbers are so high.\n【定位】They say that these prison numbers are unusually high and ask why it is the case.\n他们说这些监狱里的人数高得反常，并追问为什么会这样。\n【信号】ask why 的追问信号。录音末句 ask why it is the case 里的 ask why 是明确的\"追问原\n因\"信号；前半 these prison numbers are unusually high 交代了他们想弄清的对象\\(数量\n为何反常地高\\)，整句承载答案。\n【替换】these prison numbers are unusually high and ask why it is the case ↔\nWhy their numbers are so high\n追问数量为何反常高=问为何这么高\n【排除】A) 偷换概念\nHow they can be reduced in number\\(如何减少其数量\\)录音没说。录音只是追问\"为什\n么高\"，并未谈\"怎么把它降下来\"；本项把\"问原因\"偷换成\"找办法\"。\nC) 原词陷阱\nWhat percentage they account for 借了录音里 a high percentage of the prison\npopulation is left-handed 中的 percentage 一词。但录音用 percentage 是为了铺垫\"人\n数反常高\"、进而追问原因，呼吁者想知道的是\"为什么高\"而非\"占多少比例\"；本项用一个真\n念到的数字词诱你选偏。\n借用原文：a high percentage of the prison population is left-handed\nD) 过度推断\nIf their percentage keeps increasing\\(比例是否持续上升\\)同样盗用 percentage 一词，\n但录音从未说\"在不在增长\"，只说 unusually high\\(反常地高\\)；\"持续上升\"是录音没有的\n延伸。\n借用原文：these prison numbers are unusually high", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2021-12-1-c-26", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 26, "stem": "", "options": ["A) acknowledge", "B) assess", "C) confused", "D) endure", "E) extremely", "F) genius", "G) highly", "H) permanent", "I) possess", "J) presence", "K) puzzled", "L) status", "M) surprising", "N) thoroughly", "O) unique"], "answer": "O", "analysis": "【答案】O) unique\n形容词\n【词性槽】数词 two 与名词 individuals 之间作定语\n【依据】『两个独特的个体』呼应全文『个体特性影响关系』主线\na relationship is made of two __(26)__ individuals，下一句紧接 the personality\ntraits(特性) these individuals possess or lack 强调每个人各有不同特质——unique（独\n特的）正好点出『两个各不相同的个体』，为后文讨论个体特性如何影响关系铺垫。\n【竞争词】C) confused 义为『困惑的』，描述人状态而非个体本质，跑题\nconfused 是『困惑的』，『两个困惑的个体』与下文谈『个体特质差异』毫无关联，且\nconfused 在 29 题有死搭配 confused with，该留给 29。\nH) permanent 义为『永久的』，修饰 individuals 不通\npermanent 是『永久的』，『两个永久的个体』讲不通，个体不存在永久不永久之说。\nK) puzzled 义为『迷惑的』，与 confused 同类，跑题\npuzzled 是『困惑、迷惑的』，与 confused 近义，『两个迷惑的个体』同样接不上『个体特\n质』这条线。\nM) surprising 义为『令人惊讶的』，且 34 题有更贴切去处\nsurprising 是『令人惊讶的』，『两个令人惊讶的个体』语义突兀，且 surprising 在 34 题 not\nsurprising（不足为奇）处更贴切，应留给 34。", "points": ["选词填空", "形容词"]}, {"id": "2021-12-1-c-27", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 27, "stem": "", "options": ["A) acknowledge", "B) assess", "C) confused", "D) endure", "E) extremely", "F) genius", "G) highly", "H) permanent", "I) possess", "J) presence", "K) puzzled", "L) status", "M) surprising", "N) thoroughly", "O) unique"], "answer": "I", "analysis": "【答案】I) possess\n动词\n【词性槽】主语 these individuals 后缺谓语动词\n【依据】与 lack（缺乏）构成『拥有或缺乏』的天然反义并列\nthe personality traits(特性) these individuals __(27)__ or lack，or 后的 lack 是『缺\n乏』，or 前最自然的反义就是『拥有』——possess（拥有）与 lack 正好一正一反，『这\n些人拥有或缺乏的性格特质』逻辑通顺。\n【竞争词】A) acknowledge 义为『承认』，与 lack 不构成对照\nacknowledge 是『承认』，『承认或缺乏特质』并列别扭，且 acknowledge 在 33 题有更贴切\n的去处（acknowledge mistakes 承认错误），此处不选。\nB) assess 义为『评估』，与 lack 不成对照\nassess 是『评估』，『评估或缺乏特质』逻辑不顺，且 assess 在 31 题有更贴切的『评估自己\n缺点』之用，此处不选。\nD) endure 义为『忍受/持续』，与 lack 不成正反对照\nendure 意为『忍受、持续』，『忍受或缺乏特质』讲不通，与 lack 也不形成反义关系，且\nendure 在 28 题作『持久』正好对应『关系能否长久』。", "points": ["选词填空", "动词"]}, {"id": "2021-12-1-c-28", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 28, "stem": "", "options": ["A) acknowledge", "B) assess", "C) confused", "D) endure", "E) extremely", "F) genius", "G) highly", "H) permanent", "I) possess", "J) presence", "K) puzzled", "L) status", "M) surprising", "N) thoroughly", "O) unique"], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D) endure\n动词\n【词性槽】不定式 to 后接动词原形\n【依据】『关系能否持久』正好接 likely to endure（持续）\nmake a relationship more or less likely to __(28)__，谈的是性格特质让一段关系『更可\n能或更不可能』怎样——结合首句 passion and commitment are the foundations of\nstrong romantic relationships，这里说的是关系能否长久维系，endure（持续、持久）\n正合『一段关系是否长久』之意。\n【竞争词】A) acknowledge 『承认』需要人作主语，关系不能 acknowledge\nacknowledge 是『承认』，主语得是人；本句主语是 a relationship，关系不会『承认』什\n么，语义不通。\nB) assess 『评估』是及物动作，缺宾语且关系不会评估\nassess 是『评估』，通常要带宾语（评估某事物），而此处 to __(28)__ 后没有宾语，且关系本\n身不会『评估』，语义和结构都不通。", "points": ["选词填空", "动词"]}, {"id": "2021-12-1-c-29", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 29, "stem": "", "options": ["A) acknowledge", "B) assess", "C) confused", "D) endure", "E) extremely", "F) genius", "G) highly", "H) permanent", "I) possess", "J) presence", "K) puzzled", "L) status", "M) surprising", "N) thoroughly", "O) unique"], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C) confused\n形容词\n【词性槽】be 动词 be 后作表语\n【依据】be confused with=被混淆为，介词 with 锁死\nHumility can sometimes be __(29)__ with a lack of confidence，空后紧跟介词 with，\nbe confused with sth 是固定搭配，意为『被与……相混淆』——『谦逊有时被混淆为缺乏\n自信』，与下句 But researchers have come to realize that being humble generally\nindicates ... admirable personal qualities 的转折（其实谦逊是好品质）逻辑严丝合缝。\n【竞争词】K) puzzled 义近 confused 但不搭 with，且主语应是人\npuzzled 也表『困惑』，是最像的干扰项，但 puzzled 指『人感到困惑』、不能说『谦逊被\npuzzled with 缺乏自信』，更没有 be puzzled with 这种把两概念相混的搭配——搭配框架把\n它排除。\nH) permanent 义为『永久的』，与 with 不搭、语义不通\npermanent『永久的』代入『谦逊被永久地……缺乏自信』根本读不通，与下句转折也接不上。\nM) surprising 义为『令人惊讶的』，不与 with 搭、留给 34 题\nsurprising『令人惊讶的』无 be surprising with 一说，语义也不通，且它在 34 题 not\nsurprising 处才是正解。\n【易错】最具迷惑性的错误词是 K) —— puzzled 与正解 confused 中文同为『困惑』，词性也对，\n极易混填。Humility can sometimes be __(29)__ with a lack of confidence，puzzled 和\nconfused 词义、词性都像，很多考生凭中文一看就填 puzzled。防坑：盯空后的 with——\nbe confused with（被混淆为）是固定搭配，be puzzled with 不成立，搭配是分近义词最\n硬的尺。", "points": ["选词填空", "形容词"]}, {"id": "2021-12-1-c-30", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 30, "stem": "", "options": ["A) acknowledge", "B) assess", "C) confused", "D) endure", "E) extremely", "F) genius", "G) highly", "H) permanent", "I) possess", "J) presence", "K) puzzled", "L) status", "M) surprising", "N) thoroughly", "O) unique"], "answer": "J", "analysis": "【答案】J) presence\n名词\n【词性槽】定冠词 the 后、of 短语前作中心名词\n【依据】the presence of=……的存在，正面引出『具备好品质』\nbeing humble generally indicates the __(30)__ of deeply admirable personal\nqualities，与前半句 Humility can sometimes be confused with a lack of confidence\n的 a lack of（缺乏）形成对照——这里要说『谦逊其实表明好品质的存在』，the\npresence of（……的存在）正好与 a lack of 一缺一有相对。\n【竞争词】L) status 义为『地位』，与『好品质的存在』语义偏离\nstatus『地位、状态』代入『谦逊表明好品质的地位』讲不通——这里要的是『品质的有无』，\n与上文 a lack of 对照的应是『存在 presence』，而非『地位 status』。", "points": ["选词填空", "名词"]}, {"id": "2021-12-1-c-31", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 31, "stem": "", "options": ["A) acknowledge", "B) assess", "C) confused", "D) endure", "E) extremely", "F) genius", "G) highly", "H) permanent", "I) possess", "J) presence", "K) puzzled", "L) status", "M) surprising", "N) thoroughly", "O) unique"], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B) assess\n动词\n【词性槽】情态 can 后、副词 accurately 后接动词原形\n【依据】accurately assess（准确评估）+ 后文 honest view 呼应\naccurately __(31)__ your deficiencies without denying your skills and strengths 说的\n是『准确地□自己的不足』，下一句 humility leads to an honest view of one's own\nadvantages and shortcomings 点明这是对自身长短的『诚实评判』——assess（评估）\n正好，accurately assess 是高频搭配，『准确评估自己的缺点』。\n【竞争词】A) acknowledge 义为『承认』，但 accurately 修饰它别扭，且 33 题更需要它\nacknowledge 是『承认』，可与 accurately 搭配略显牵强（承认通常不论『准确』），更关键\n的是后文 They can __(33)__ mistakes『承认错误』非 acknowledge 莫属，acknowledge 该\n留给 33 题，此处用 assess 更贴 accurately。", "points": ["选词填空", "动词"]}, {"id": "2021-12-1-c-32", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 32, "stem": "", "options": ["A) acknowledge", "B) assess", "C) confused", "D) endure", "E) extremely", "F) genius", "G) highly", "H) permanent", "I) possess", "J) presence", "K) puzzled", "L) status", "M) surprising", "N) thoroughly", "O) unique"], "answer": "F", "analysis": "【答案】F) genius\n名词\n【词性槽】不定冠词 a 后只能接名词\n【依据】与前句『intelligent』构成『聪明但算不上天才』的递进对比\n上文 you might recognize that you are intelligent, but realize that you are not a\n__(32)__，but 前说『你也许聪明』，but 后该说一个比『聪明』更高一档、自己却够不上\n的身份——天才 genius 正好，承认聪明却不自封天才，恰好呼应『谦逊=客观看待自己』\n的主旨。\n【竞争词】J) presence 语义不搭，『你不是一个存在』不通\npresence 意为『存在、在场』，代入『you are not a presence』读不通，跟前文\n『intelligent』毫无对比关系，逻辑上接不上。\nL) status 指地位而非『一类人』，与『聪明』不构成对比\nstatus 是『地位、状态』，但 but 前的 intelligent 描述的是个人才智，转折后该接一个表示\n『更高才智级别的人』的词，status 指地位，对不上这条才智递进线，且后文 34、35 空更需要\n它充当名词候选作干扰。", "points": ["选词填空", "名词"]}, {"id": "2021-12-1-c-33", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 33, "stem": "", "options": ["A) acknowledge", "B) assess", "C) confused", "D) endure", "E) extremely", "F) genius", "G) highly", "H) permanent", "I) possess", "J) presence", "K) puzzled", "L) status", "M) surprising", "N) thoroughly", "O) unique"], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A) acknowledge\n动词\n【词性槽】情态 can 后接动词原形、带宾语 mistakes\n【依据】acknowledge mistakes=承认错误，固定搭配且贴合谦逊主旨\nThey can __(33)__ mistakes, see value in things that are far from perfect，谦逊的人能\n『承认错误、看到不完美事物的价值』——acknowledge mistakes（承认错误）是地道固\n定搭配，与本段『谦逊者不回避、不否认自身缺陷』完全一致。", "points": ["选词填空", "动词"]}, {"id": "2021-12-1-c-34", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 34, "stem": "", "options": ["A) acknowledge", "B) assess", "C) confused", "D) endure", "E) extremely", "F) genius", "G) highly", "H) permanent", "I) possess", "J) presence", "K) puzzled", "L) status", "M) surprising", "N) thoroughly", "O) unique"], "answer": "M", "analysis": "【答案】M) surprising\n形容词\n【词性槽】it is not + 空，作表语形容词\n【依据】not surprising=不足为奇，承接上文顺理成章\nPerhaps it is not __(34)__, then, that humility appears to be a huge asset to\nrelationships，then（因此）承接第二段对谦逊种种好处的铺陈，得出『谦逊对关系大有裨\n益，这并不令人意外』——it is not surprising that...（……并不奇怪）是高频句式，逻辑上\n水到渠成。\n【竞争词】K) puzzled 义为『（人）困惑的』，不能修饰事/作 it 的表语\npuzzled 形容人感到困惑，主语须是人，不能说『it is not puzzled that...』，结构上就排除。\nH) permanent 义为『永久的』，代入 it is not permanent that 语义不通\npermanent『永久的』代入『谦逊有益于关系这件事并非永久的』，与 then 承接的因果语气、\n与全文论证都不搭。", "points": ["选词填空", "形容词"]}, {"id": "2021-12-1-c-35", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 35, "stem": "", "options": ["A) acknowledge", "B) assess", "C) confused", "D) endure", "E) extremely", "F) genius", "G) highly", "H) permanent", "I) possess", "J) presence", "K) puzzled", "L) status", "M) surprising", "N) thoroughly", "O) unique"], "answer": "G", "analysis": "【答案】G) highly\n副词\n【词性槽】动词 rate 后修饰动作，作状语\n【依据】rate ... highly=高度评价，固定搭配\npeople tend to rate this quality __(35)__ in their spouse，rate sth highly 是固定搭配，\n意为『高度评价某物』——『人们往往非常看重配偶身上谦逊这一品质』，与下文『谦逊的\n伴侣或许是你的理想伴侣』正向呼应。\n【竞争词】E) extremely 义为『极其』，不与 rate 搭配作『评价高』\nextremely『极其』一般修饰形容词/副词（extremely good），不能说 rate sth extremely 表\n示『高度评价』；它要修饰的是程度形容词，这里 rate 后没有形容词可修饰，搭配不成立。\nN) thoroughly 义为『彻底地』，与『评价高』方向不符\nthoroughly『彻底地、完全地』修饰的是『做得彻底』（thoroughly clean），rate sth\nthoroughly 表示『彻底地评价』而非『评价很高』，与下文『理想伴侣』的褒扬语气对不上。\n【易错】最具迷惑性的错误词是 E) —— extremely 与正解 highly 都译『非常』，词性也对，最易\n误选。rate this quality __(35)__，extremely 副词且中文同『非常』，看着也能填。防坑：\n记住 rate sth highly 是『高度评价』的固定搭配，而 extremely 必须后接形容词/副词，\nrate 后无形容词，extremely 无处落脚。", "points": ["选词填空", "副词"]}, {"id": "2021-12-1-m-36", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 36, "stem": "Some career women who aim high tend to feel guilty if they fail to achieve their goals.", "options": null, "answer": "H", "analysis": "【答案】H 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】career women aim high + fail + feel guilty\n【定位】Some professional women aim to do it all. They want to reach the top of the corporate\nladder and fly like supermom. When women don't reach this ideal, they feel guilty; and even\nmore stressed.\n一些职业女性想把所有事都做到——既要爬上公司顶层、又要像超级妈妈一样飞起来。当她\n们没能达到这个理想时，她们会感到内疚，压力反而更大。\n【改写】Some career women who aim high ↔ Some professional women aim to do it all\n同义替换：aim high ≈ aim to do it all\nif they fail to achieve their goals ↔ When women don't reach this ideal\n同义替换：fail to achieve ≈ don't reach\ntend to feel guilty ↔ they feel guilty\n原样复现", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2021-12-1-m-37", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 37, "stem": "The unpaid housework done by women is triple that done by men.", "options": null, "answer": "E", "analysis": "【答案】E 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】unpaid housework + triple\n【定位】For example, the United Nations reported that women do nearly three times as much unpaid\ndomestic work as men.\n例如，联合国报告说女性承担的无偿家务劳动几乎是男性的三倍。\n【改写】The unpaid housework done by women ↔\nwomen do nearly three times as much unpaid domestic work\n同义替换：housework ≈ domestic work\nis triple that done by men ↔ nearly three times as much unpaid domestic work as men\n数量倍数的同义改写：triple ≈ three times", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2021-12-1-m-38", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 38, "stem": "It is reported that women consistently suffer more from severe stress than men.", "options": null, "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】reported + women consistently + severe stress\n【定位】The American Psychological Association reports a gender gap year after year showing that\nwomen consistently report higher stress levels.\n美国心理学会年复一年地报告存在一种性别差距，显示女性持续报告更高的压力水平。\n【改写】It is reported that ↔\nThe American Psychological Association reports a gender gap year after year\n被动\"被报告\"对应主动\"APA 报告说\"\nwomen consistently suffer more from severe stress than men ↔\nwomen consistently report higher stress levels\n同义替换：suffer more from severe stress ≈ report higher stress levels\n【辨邻】 D It's been well documented in extensive research over the years that prevalence\nrates for the majority of the anxiety disorders are higher in women than men 谈\n的是\"研究中已有记录\"，但具体数据和\"年复一年报告\"的源头还是 C 段，D 段属于话题\n相邻的解读，不是\"被报告\"原句。\nIt's been well documented in extensive research over the years that prevalence rates for the majority\nof the anxiety disorders are higher in women than men", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2021-12-1-m-39", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 39, "stem": "Women are advised to identify the specific causes of their stress so that steps can be taken to deal with it.", "options": null, "answer": "K", "analysis": "【答案】K 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】identify specific causes of stress\n【定位】It is important to understand what causes your stress in the first place. \"Get really specific\nwith what's stressing you out,\" Ms. Li said.\n首先弄清楚是什么造成了你的压力，这一点很重要。Li 女士说：\"对到底是什么让你有压\n力，要弄得非常具体。\"\n【改写】Women are advised to identify the specific causes of their stress ↔\nGet really specific with what's stressing you out\n祈使句⇄被动建议句的句式转换\nso that steps can be taken to deal with it ↔\nThese are different causes of stress and can benefit from different kinds of self-care\n上位概括：不同自我照护 ⊆ 采取措施处理\n【辨邻】 J J 段建议的是 The basics of adequate sleep, healthy diet and exercise 与 Support\nfrom trusted relationships is vital，没有提到\"识别具体压力源\"这件事，只是话题相\n邻的另一类建议。\nThe basics of adequate sleep, healthy diet and exercise are a good place to start", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2021-12-1-m-40", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 40, "stem": "One study showed that women managers often expressed positive emotions that they didn't really feel.", "options": null, "answer": "F", "analysis": "【答案】F 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】women managers + positive emotions + didn't really feel\n【定位】Research from Nova Southeastern University found that female managers were more likely\nthan male managers to display \"surface acting,\" or forcing emotions that are not wholly felt.\n\"They expressed optimism, calmness and sympathy even when these were not the emotions\nthat they were actually feeling,\" the study said.\nNova Southeastern 大学的研究发现，女性管理者比男性管理者更倾向于\"表层表演\"，也就\n是强行表现出并非完全真切感受到的情绪。研究称：\"她们表现出乐观、镇定和同情，即便\n这些并不是她们实际感受到的情绪。\"\n【改写】women managers ↔ female managers\n同义替换：women ≈ female\noften expressed positive emotions ↔ They expressed optimism, calmness and sympathy\n上位概括：positive emotions ⊇ optimism/calmness/sympathy\nthey didn't really feel ↔ not the emotions that they were actually feeling\n同义替换：didn't really feel ≈ not actually feeling\n【辨邻】 G Surface acting is a prime example of emotional labor 借\"表层表演\"引出新概念，\n是延伸讨论，但 One study showed 指的是 Nova Southeastern 大学那项研究本\n身，源头仍在 F 段。\nSurface acting is a prime example of emotional labor", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2021-12-1-m-41", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 41, "stem": "Women tend to mistake signs of heart attacks for symptoms of stress.", "options": null, "answer": "I", "analysis": "【答案】I 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】mistake signs of heart attacks for symptoms of stress\n【定位】For example, women are more likely to experience disturbed sleep, anxiety and unusual\nfatigue before a heart attack. Stress is so normalized that it is easy for women to shrug off\nthose symptoms as simply the consequences of stress.\n例如，女性比男性更容易在心脏病发作前出现睡眠紊乱、焦虑和异常疲劳。由于压力被视为\n常态，女性很容易把这些症状当成不过是压力的副产品而不当回事。\n【改写】signs of heart attacks ↔ disturbed sleep, anxiety and unusual fatigue before a heart attack\n上位概括：signs of heart attacks ⊇ disturbed sleep/anxiety/unusual fatigue\nmistake signs of heart attacks for symptoms of stress ↔\nshrug off those symptoms as simply the consequences of stress\n动作等值：mistake A for B ≈ shrug off A as B", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2021-12-1-m-42", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 42, "stem": "For a time an app developer in America was so busy attending to work and family that she suffered from sleeplessness.", "options": null, "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】app developer in America + work + family + sleeplessness\n【定位】Ms. Li said her stress led to sleeplessness. When she did sleep, she experienced \"problem-\nsolving dreams,\" which left her feeling unrested when she woke up. \"After I became a first-\ntime mother, I quickly realized I was so busy caring for other people and work that I felt like\nI'd lost myself,\" she said.\nLi 女士说她的压力让她失眠。当她真的睡着时，她会做\"解题梦\"，醒来仍感到没休息好。她\n说：\"当上新手妈妈之后，我很快意识到自己忙着照顾别人和工作，感觉迷失了自己。\"\n【改写】an app developer in America ↔ Ms. Li said her stress led to sleeplessness\n回指等值：Ms. Li = 上文已介绍的 app developer\nso busy attending to work and family that she suffered from sleeplessness ↔\nher stress led to sleeplessness\n同义替换：suffered from sleeplessness ≈ stress led to sleeplessness\nattending to work and family ↔ so busy caring for other people and work\n同义替换：attending to ≈ caring for；family ≈ other people\n【辨邻】 A I love to create things, grow them and solve problems 与 I didn't really care\nabout my mind and my body until they decided to go on strike 只展示她工作热\n情和身心崩溃的暗示，没有\"sleeplessness\"和\"work and family\"具体细节，只能算只\n覆盖局部。\na successful app developer in San Francisco", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2021-12-1-m-43", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 43, "stem": "The emotional labor women do is noticed only when it is not done.", "options": null, "answer": "G", "analysis": "【答案】G 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】emotional labor + noticed only when not done\n【定位】These invisible duties become apparent only when you don't do them.\n这些看不见的职责，只有在你不做的时候才会被察觉。\n【改写】The emotional labor women do ↔\nemotional labor: the duties that are expected of them, but go unnoticed\n原样复现 + 局部解释\nis noticed only when it is not done ↔\nThese invisible duties become apparent only when you don't do them\n同义替换：noticed ≈ become apparent；it is not done ≈ you don't do them\n【辨邻】 F female managers were more likely than male managers to display surface\nacting 把焦点放在\"表层表演\"，是 G 段所说 emotional labor 的下位例，但不承\n载\"只有不做才被注意\"这一陈述。\nResearch from Nova Southeastern University found that female managers were more likely than\nmale managers to display surface acting", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2021-12-1-m-44", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 44, "stem": "Dr. Joyce suggests that apart from self-care, women should seek professional support if they experience severe stress.", "options": null, "answer": "J", "analysis": "【答案】J 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】Dr. Joyce + apart from self-care + professional support + severe stress\n【定位】Support from trusted relationships is vital. This includes professional support from various\nhealth and wellness providers if stress is becoming increasingly overwhelming.\n来自可信关系的支持至关重要，这包括在压力越来越压垮人时寻求各种健康与身心保健专业\n人士的支持。\n【改写】Dr. Joyce suggests that ↔ Dr. Joyce said\n言说动词同义：suggests ≈ said（在 give advice 语境）\napart from self-care, women should seek professional support ↔\nThe basics of adequate sleep, healthy diet and exercise are a good place to start This includes\nprofessional support from various health and wellness providers\n递进关系等值：apart from self-care ≈ self-care 之外再加 professional support\nif they experience severe stress ↔ if stress is becoming increasingly overwhelming\n条件等值：severe stress ≈ stress is becoming increasingly overwhelming\n【辨邻】 L Women working outside of the home should make an effort to have a\nconscious conversation with their partners about more equitable sharing of\nhousehold and family responsibilities 只涉及与伴侣沟通分担责任，不是\nprofessional support 的建议。\nhave a conscious conversation with their partners about more equitable sharing of household and\nfamily responsibilities", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2021-12-1-m-45", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 45, "stem": "Some people believe that there may not exist a stress gap between men and women.", "options": null, "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】may not exist + stress gap between men and women\n【定位】Some people may argue that this is merely reported data, and they say many men feel the\nsame pressures as women in terms of fulfilling responsibilities at work and home. In other\nwords, we're all really, really stressed.\n有些人或许会反驳说这只不过是被报告出来的数据，并称许多男性在履行工作与家庭责任时\n也承受着与女性同样的压力。换句话说，我们都真的真的很有压力。\n【改写】Some people believe ↔ Some people may argue\n同义替换：believe ≈ argue（持有/主张观点）\nmay not exist a stress gap between men and women ↔\nmany men feel the same pressures as women in terms of fulfilling responsibilities at work and\nhome\n语义反推等值：男女都同样有压力 ⇒ 差距不存在\nmay not exist a stress gap between men and women ↔\nIn other words, we're all really, really stressed\n总结句把反方立场显化：\"我们都同样有压力\"\n【辨邻】 C Clearly, a stress gap exists 是对差距存在的正面肯定，立场与 may not exist a\nstress gap 完全相反；C 的所有数据都在支持\"差距存在\"，因此不是反方立场段。\nClearly, a stress gap exists", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2021-12-1-r-46", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 46, "stem": "What does the author say about working from home?", "options": ["A) It will become the norm sooner or later.", "B) It requires employees to adapt promptly.", "C) It benefits employers at the expense of employees.", "D) It will force cities to transform their infrastructure."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题）\n【判型】What does the author say about working from home? 没有 infer/imply/mainly\nabout，而是问作者就 working from home 这一具体话题说了什么，落点在一处可定位的\n判断句，因此按细节题处理。\n【拆句】题干 What does the author say about working from home? 主干是 What does the\nauthor say，about working from home 是 say 的介词宾语状语，无从句、无否定、无比\n较，结构平铺直叙，无陷阱。\n【定位】Employers argue they make considerable savings on real estate when workers shift from\noffice to home work. However, these savings result from passing costs on to workers.\n雇主声称：员工从办公室转向居家办公时，他们能在地产上省下相当一笔。然而，这些节省\n其实来自把成本转嫁给员工。\n【选项】A) 把假设说成必然\nIt will become the norm sooner or later. 把 if working from home becomes the\nnorm 的假设条件硬改成确定预测，作者只是在假设语境下讨论后果，并未断言它必将\n成为常态。\nB) 无中生有\nIt requires employees to adapt promptly. 中的 require 与 promptly 在文中无任何对\n应；首段虽提 office workers adapt to remote work，但并未提出“要求迅速适应”的规\n定性命题。\nC) ✓ 正确\nEmployers argue they make considerable savings on real estate ↔ It benefits employers\nthese savings result from passing costs on to workers ↔ at the expense of employees\n预判到的“雇主受益、员工承担”两段恰好被 It benefits employers at the expense of\nemployees. 同时覆盖，是定位句两部分的同义合并。\nD) 过度推断\nIt will force cities to transform their infrastructure. 中的 force 与 transform their\ninfrastructure 远超原文 cities may undergo fundamental change 的措辞——文中只\n是说城市可能发生根本性变化，并未具体到基建改造，更未说“被迫”。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2021-12-1-r-47", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 47, "stem": "Why do some people oppose working from home?", "options": ["A) It discourages team spirit.", "B) It invades employees' privacy.", "C) It undermines traditional values.", "D) It negatively impacts productivity."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题）\n【判型】Why do some people oppose working from home? 用 why 直接问反对原因，答案是文\n中明确列举的事实理由，可在一句话里找到，按细节题处理。\n【拆句】题干 Why do some people oppose working from home? 是标准 why 引导的疑问句，\nworking from home 是 oppose 的动名词宾语，结构清晰，无陷阱。\n【定位】Though employers are backed by a chorus of remote work advocates, others note the\nloneliness, reduced productivity and inefficiencies of extended remote work.\n尽管雇主有一群远程办公拥护者助阵，也有人指出长期远程办公带来的孤独感、生产力下降\n和低效问题。\n【选项】A) 张冠李戴\nIt discourages team spirit. 与 team-building 有形近，但 team-building 出现在尾段\n讲办公室优势的语境里，并非反对方的理由；原文反对方提的是 loneliness 而不是\nteam spirit。\nB) 无中生有\nIt invades employees' privacy. 中的 invades privacy 在文中无对应；dedicate part\nof their private space to work 只是说要在私人空间里腾地方，并非侵犯隐私。\nC) 偷换概念\nIt undermines traditional values. 中的 traditional values 在原文完全没出现；\ntraditionally been paid by the employer 里的 traditionally 是说“历来由雇主支付”，\n跟“传统价值观”风马牛不相及。\nD) ✓ 正确\nreduced productivity ↔ negatively impacts productivity\n并列三项里 reduced productivity 与 negatively impacts productivity productivity\n一词原词复现，且 reduced 与 negatively impacts 极性一致，是最干净的同义替换。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2021-12-1-r-48", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 48, "stem": "Why did the author run the estimates for Montreal?", "options": ["A) To provide convincing data for serious discussion.", "B) To illustrate the ongoing change in working patterns.", "C) To show the impact of remote working on productivity.", "D) To exemplify how remote working affects the economy."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题）\n【判型】Why did the author run the estimates for Montreal? 问的是作者具体动作的目的，原文\n一般在动作句的同一句或紧邻句用 since/so as to/in order to 直接给出，按细节题处理。\n【拆句】题干 Why did the author run the estimates for Montreal? 是 why 引导的一般疑问句过\n去时，for Montreal 作 estimates 的修饰短语，结构简单，无陷阱。\n【定位】The exercise is simple but important, since it brings these costs out of the realm of\nspeculation into the realm of meaningful discussion.\n这项估算操作虽简单却重要，因为它把这些成本从空想范畴推进到可作有意义讨论的范畴。\n【选项】A) ✓ 正确\nbrings these costs out of the realm of speculation ↔ convincing data\ninto the realm of meaningful discussion ↔ serious discussion\n选项 To provide convincing data for serious discussion. 把 since 子句的两段（出空\n想→入严肃讨论）逐一对应，是最干净的同义替换。\nB) 答非所问\nTo illustrate the ongoing change in working patterns. 中的 change in working\npatterns 在原文是话题背景，不是作者做估算的目的；since 子句里完全没有这一\n含义。\nC) 张冠李戴\nTo show the impact of remote working on productivity. 把 reduced productivity\n的子话题硬装成估算目的；蒙特利尔估算估的是成本，不是生产力。\nD) 过度推断\nTo exemplify how remote working affects the economy. 把作者只为蒙特利尔一城做\n的成本估算拔高为“对经济的影响”，原文 since 子句只说要推进 meaningful\ndiscussion，没有提 the economy。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2021-12-1-r-49", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 49, "stem": "What can we conclude from the author's calculations?", "options": ["A) There is no point in transferring office work to working from home.", "B) Employees can benefit as much from remote working as their employers.", "C) Employers' gain from remote working should go to employees as compensation.", "D) Effective measures should be taken to motivate employees to set up offices at home."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （推断题）\n【判型】What can we conclude from the author's calculations? 用 conclude，要求由文内信息\n推出一个未直接写出的结论，但只能一步延伸，属于轻推断题。\n【拆句】题干 What can we conclude from the author's calculations? 是 wh-疑问句，from the\nauthor's calculations 是 conclude 的来源状语，结构简单，无陷阱。\n【定位】Rough calculations show that the savings made by employers when their staff works from\nhome are of similar value to the compensation workers should receive for setting up offices\nat home.\n粗略计算表明，员工居家办公时雇主所节省的费用，与员工为在家设立办公空间应得的补偿\n金额相当。\n【选项】A) 过度推断\nThere is no point in transferring office work to working from home. 用 no point\nin 全盘否定居家办公，原文只说省额会被补偿抵消，并未说居家办公毫无意义；这是把\n作者的有限主张推过头。\nB) 偷换概念\nEmployees can benefit as much from remote working as their employers. 中的 as\nmuch as 等同关系在原文无对应；原文是说雇主省的需要全数补偿员工，并未推出员工\n获得等额收益。\nC) ✓ 正确\nthe savings made by employers ↔ Employers' gain from remote working\nare of similar value to the compensation workers should receive ↔\nshould go to employees as compensation\n预判到的“雇主省的≈员工该被补的→所以这笔钱应转给员工”恰好被 Employers' gain\nfrom remote working should go to employees as compensation. 完整覆盖，是教\n科书式的一步推断。\nD) 与原文相反\nEffective measures should be taken to motivate employees to set up offices at\nhome. 中的 motivate 是规定性命题，原文从未提出任何鼓励员工建家庭办公室的政策\n建议；恰恰相反，作者偏向回办公室。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "推断题"]}, {"id": "2021-12-1-r-50", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 50, "stem": "What is the author's opinion on working from home?", "options": ["A) It should be avoided if possible.", "B) It is only a temporary measure.", "C) It can reduce companies' real estate costs.", "D) It may affect employees' corporate loyalty."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （态度题）\n【判型】What is the author's opinion on working from home? 直接用 opinion 询问作者态度，\n需要聚合全文极性词，按态度题特殊路径处理。\n【拆句】题干 What is the author's opinion on working from home? 主干是 What is the\nauthor's opinion，on working from home 修饰 opinion，限定话题范围，结构简单，无\n陷阱。\n【选项】A) ✓ 正确\nshelve the idea of remote work ↔ It should be avoided if possible.\nparasitic(寄生的) capitalism ↔ should be avoided\n全文从 parasitic→expropriation→shelve 一路负向收束，无一处肯定，It should be\navoided if possible. 是唯一与主线全篇极性一致的选项。\nB) 无中生有\nIt is only a temporary measure. 中的 temporary 在原文没有任何对应词；作者讨论的\n是若成为常态会怎样，并未把它定性为“临时措施”。\nC) 张冠李戴\nIt can reduce companies' real estate costs. 是雇主的说法 Employers argue they\nmake considerable savings on real estate，但作者随即反驳并在尾段说 employers'\nreal estate savings will be modest——所以这不是作者的观点。\nD) 无中生有\nIt may affect employees' corporate loyalty. 中的 corporate loyalty 在文中完全没出\n现，作者关心的是成本转嫁与办公室优势，不涉及忠诚度问题。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "态度题"]}, {"id": "2021-12-1-r-51", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 51, "stem": "What does the author say about curiosity?", "options": ["A) It is too complex for non-scientists to understand.", "B) It is the force that pushes human society forward.", "C) It is a unique trait specific to the human race.", "D) It is often the major cause for human failures."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题）\n【判型】题干 What does the author say about curiosity? 询问作者明确陈述的内容，而非暗含意\n思或主旨概括，属于针对原文具体表述的细节题。\n【拆句】题干 What does the author say about curiosity? 是简单特殊疑问句，主干 the author\nsay 接 about curiosity 介词短语作状语，无从句、无否定、无比较，结构平铺直叙、无\n陷阱。\n【定位】The human thirst for knowledge is the driving force behind our successful development as\na species.\n人类对知识的渴求是我们作为一个物种成功发展背后的驱动力。\n【选项】A) 偷换概念\n原文 scientists have found it hard to define 只说科学家觉得它难定义，并未说非科学\n家无法理解；too complex for non-scientists to understand 把对象从“科学家”悄悄换\n成“非科学家”、把“难定义”夸大为“无法理解”。\nB) ✓ 正确\nthe driving force behind our successful development as a species ↔\nthe force that pushes human society forward\nour successful development as a species ↔ human society\n预判“好奇心推动人类整体发展”与 It is the force that pushes human society forward\n在语义上完全重合，是定位句最干净的同义替换。\nC) 无中生有\n原文从未说好奇心是“人类专有的特征”，P5 反而提到 non-human animals 也会探索新\n事物，a unique trait specific to the human race 与原文相反，属无中生有兼与原文\n相反。\nD) 过度推断\n原文 curiosity can also be dangerous, leading to setbacks or even downfalls 用\nalso 引出次要补充，often the major cause for human failures 把\nsetbacks/downfalls 升级为 the major cause，并加上频率词 often，过度推断。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2021-12-1-r-52", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 52, "stem": "What is the general understanding of curiosity?", "options": ["A) It motivates people to seek information.", "B) It is destined to transform human genes.", "C) It does people more good than harm.", "D) It underlies all human behaviors."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题）\n【判型】题干 What is the general understanding of curiosity? 只问大家的共识内容是什么，属于\n客观信息检索类细节题。\n【拆句】题干 What is the general understanding of curiosity? 是“What is 主语？”句型，主语\nthe general understanding of curiosity 是名词词组，of 短语作后置定语限定\nunderstanding，无陷阱。\n【定位】While pinning down a definition has proven tricky, the general consensus is it's some means\nof information gathering.\n尽管要给出确切定义被证明很棘手，普遍共识是好奇心是某种信息收集的手段。\n【选项】A) ✓ 正确\nsome means of information gathering ↔ motivates people to seek information\n预判“好奇心是信息收集的手段”与 It motivates people to seek information 在功能上\n等价：好奇心驱动人去寻找/收集信息。\nB) 无中生有\n原文 curiosity does have a genetic component 只说好奇心有遗传成分，并未说好奇\n心“注定要改变人类基因”；destined to transform human genes 不仅方向反转，还把\n名词性的“遗传成分”改成动词性的“改造基因”，完全无据。\nC) 无中生有\n原文 can also be dangerous 与 But curiosity often comes with a cost 都明示了好奇\n心的代价，并未给出 “好处大于坏处” 的比较结论；does people more good than\nharm 凭空加上比较结构。\nD) 以偏概全\n原文 some means of information gathering 只说好奇心是信息收集的“某种”手段，\nunderlies all human behaviors 把范围夸大到“支配全部人类行为”，远超原文范围。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2021-12-1-r-53", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 53, "stem": "What do we learn about how genes shape people's behavior?", "options": ["A) They determine people's way of thinking.", "B) They account for age differences in learning.", "C) They enable people to undertake massive tasks.", "D) They work in conjunction with the environment."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题）\n【判型】题干 What do we learn about how genes shape people's behavior? 问的是文中明示的\n机理而非推断，属细节题。\n【拆句】题干 What do we learn about how genes shape people's behavior? 主干是 What do\nwe learn about，about 后跟 how 引导的宾语从句 how genes shape people's behavior\n——how 在此是“以何种方式”，不是“是否”，提问点落在“机理”而不是“结果存在与否”。\n【定位】Genes and the environment interact in many complex ways to shape individuals and guide\ntheir behavior, including their curiosity.\n基因和环境以许多复杂的方式相互作用，塑造个体并指导他们的行为，包括他们的好奇心。\n【选项】A) 偷换概念\n原文谈的是 behavior（行为），选项 way of thinking 偷换为思维方式；且 determine\n的力度比 shape and guide 强，原文也强调“与环境互动”，并非基因单方“决定”。\nB) 无中生有\n原文没有讨论学习中的年龄差异，age differences in learning 完全是凭空添加的\n话题。\nC) 张冠李戴\n原文 infants have to learn an incredible amount of information in a short time 描\n述的是婴儿任务量大，并未说基因让人“承担巨大任务”；enable people to undertake\nmassive tasks 把对象张冠李戴。\nD) ✓ 正确\nGenes and the environment interact in many complex ways ↔\nThey work in conjunction with the environment\nshape individuals and guide their behavior ↔ work\n预判“基因与环境共同起作用”与 They work in conjunction with the environment 在\n主语、对象、动作方向上一一对应。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2021-12-1-r-54", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 54, "stem": "What do numerous studies show about infants?", "options": ["A) They are far more curious than adults.", "B) They prefer to go after all that is novel.", "C) They have different interests than adults.", "D) They show non-human animal behaviors."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题）\n【判型】题干 What do numerous studies show about infants? 问研究“表明了什么”，是对原文研\n究结论的直接询问，属细节题。\n【拆句】题干 What do numerous studies show about infants? 是“What do A show about B?”\n句型，主语 numerous studies、宾语为 What、关于 infants，提问点是“关于婴儿的研究\n结论”，无陷阱。\n【定位】Hundreds of studies show that infants prefer novelty.\n数以百计的研究表明，婴儿偏爱新奇事物。\n【选项】A) 无中生有\n原文没有对婴儿与成人做“好奇程度比较”，只在末尾说 probably human adults 也会探\n索；far more curious than adults 凭空加上比较级 + 程度副词 far more。\nB) ✓ 正确\ninfants prefer novelty ↔ They prefer to go after all that is novel\n预判“婴儿偏爱新奇事物”与 They prefer to go after all that is novel 几乎逐字对应，是\n定位句最干净的同义替换。\nC) 偷换概念\n原文只说婴儿偏好新奇事物，并没有比较婴儿与成人的兴趣范围；different interests\nthan adults 偷换为“兴趣点的差异”，与原文研究结论不在一个维度。\nD) 偷换概念\n原文 non-human animals, human infants and probably human adults 把动物、婴\n儿、成人并列为同样会“偏好新奇”的群体，并非说婴儿“表现出非人类动物的行为”；\nshow non-human animal behaviors 把并列关系曲解为附属关系。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2021-12-1-r-55", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 55, "stem": "What does the example of the Inuit people of the Arctic regions illustrate?", "options": ["A) The cost of humans' curiosity to explore.", "B) The incredible harshness of cold weather.", "C) The innovative ideas stemming from curiosity.", "D) The importance of learning from past failures."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （例证目的题）\n【判型】题干 What does the example of the Inuit people of the Arctic regions illustrate? 明确\n把 the Inuit people 标记为 the example，问其“说明了什么”，是典型的例证目的题。\n【拆句】题干 What does the example of the Inuit people of the Arctic regions illustrate? 主干\nthe example illustrate，其中 of the Inuit people of the Arctic regions 嵌套两层 of 短语\n作 example 的后置定语；考生易把宾语错认为“因纽特人本身”，实际宾语是 What，即“这\n个例子说明了什么”。\n【定位】But curiosity often comes with a cost.\n但好奇心往往是有代价的。\n【选项】A) ✓ 正确\nBut curiosity often comes with a cost ↔ The cost of humans' curiosity to explore\ntens of thousands of people that tried and failed ↔ to explore\n预判“好奇心要付出代价”与 The cost of humans' curiosity to explore 在“代价 + 好奇\n心 + 探索”三要素上完全对应。\nB) 答非所问\nThe incredible harshness of cold weather 只是 living in northern climates 的背景\n描述，是例子里的细节，而非例子要支撑的论点；例证目的题不能选例子内部的表层\n细节。\nC) 以偏概全\n原文 created incredible modes to deal with the challenges 与 tens of thousands\nof people that tried and failed 是一组对比，选项 innovative ideas stemming from\ncuriosity 只看到前者“创新成果”，忽略后者“代价”——而后者才是本段真正想强调的部\n分（由 but what we forget about 引出）。\nD) 偷换概念\n原文 But curiosity often comes with a cost 与 tens of thousands of people that\ntried and failed 只强调“代价/牺牲”这一客观事实，没有谈“从过往失败中学习”的主观行\n动；learning from past failures 把客观代价偷换为主观学习行为。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "例证目的题"]}]});
